00:03:02.841 --> 00:03:06.375
on these topics in my lab
and applications and so on.

00:03:06.376 --> 00:03:08.070
All right, and by the way,

00:03:08.071 --> 00:03:09.790
do ask questions along the way.

00:03:09.791 --> 00:03:12.080
So, imperfect-information
games are

00:03:12.081 --> 00:03:14.820
very unlike Chess or Go.

00:03:14.821 --> 00:03:16.700
Where in Chess or Go you

00:03:16.701 --> 00:03:19.080
know exactly what
the state of the world is.

00:03:19.081 --> 00:03:21.535
In Imperfect-information
games you don't.

00:03:21.536 --> 00:03:23.870
They're much more like
negotiation, or poker,

00:03:23.871 --> 00:03:27.560
or military settings, or
video games and so forth.

00:03:27.561 --> 00:03:29.900
So, you all have probably heard

00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:32.135
about AlphaGo and AlphaZero.

00:03:32.136 --> 00:03:35.560
It's a great success story
and a very complex game.

00:03:35.561 --> 00:03:38.120
Those techniques can in principle

00:03:38.121 --> 00:03:40.410
apply to other perfect
information games,

00:03:40.411 --> 00:03:43.140
and in fact, they have
shown very good success.

00:03:43.141 --> 00:03:45.445
For example, in Chess,
and Shogi and so on.

00:03:45.446 --> 00:03:46.820
However, they don't apply to

00:03:46.821 --> 00:03:49.185
Imperfect-information
games at all.

00:03:49.186 --> 00:03:52.510
So, in Perfect-information games,

00:03:52.511 --> 00:03:54.550
you can solve a sub-game of

00:03:54.551 --> 00:03:57.755
the game tree based on information
in the sub-game alone.

00:03:57.756 --> 00:03:59.305
So, for example, in Chess,

00:03:59.306 --> 00:04:01.040
how you play
the Sicilian defense is

00:04:01.041 --> 00:04:02.330
completely independent of how

00:04:02.331 --> 00:04:04.475
you should play
the Queen's gambit.

00:04:04.476 --> 00:04:08.270
This is not true in
Imperfect-information games,

00:04:08.271 --> 00:04:10.520
and to highlight this difference

00:04:10.521 --> 00:04:12.780
let me run through an example.

00:04:12.781 --> 00:04:15.400
This is a small example
we call, Coin toss.

00:04:15.401 --> 00:04:18.500
This also shows a general
representation of how do you

00:04:18.501 --> 00:04:21.190
represent
Imperfect-information games in

00:04:21.191 --> 00:04:22.435
a game independent way.

00:04:22.436 --> 00:04:24.190
This is called the
extensive form,

00:04:24.191 --> 00:04:26.685
which is a standard
representation in game theory.

00:04:26.686 --> 00:04:29.675
All right, so, how this
works, this example,

00:04:29.676 --> 00:04:31.520
and by the way this
is a tiny game

00:04:31.521 --> 00:04:32.980
just for illustrative purposes,

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:34.795
the real games are huge.

00:04:34.796 --> 00:04:37.120
So, we have three players,

00:04:37.121 --> 00:04:39.890
there's Chance, Player
1 and Player 2.

00:04:39.891 --> 00:04:41.340
Chance really just acts

00:04:41.341 --> 00:04:43.120
stochastically,
not strategically.

00:04:43.121 --> 00:04:45.780
Let's say in this game
Chance just flips a coin,

00:04:45.781 --> 00:04:47.590
heads or tails, 50-50.

00:04:47.591 --> 00:04:51.000
Then Player 1 can either
sell the coin or play,

00:04:51.001 --> 00:04:53.030
and if Player 1 plays,

00:04:53.031 --> 00:04:54.925
then Player 2 gets a move,

00:04:54.926 --> 00:04:58.660
gets to guess which
way the coin came out.

00:04:58.661 --> 00:05:01.120
Heads, tails, or
Player 2 can just

00:05:01.121 --> 00:05:02.480
give up and say,
"Well, I don't know.

00:05:02.481 --> 00:05:06.060
You win." The payoffs

00:05:06.061 --> 00:05:08.925
are shown from
Player 1's perspective.

00:05:08.926 --> 00:05:11.960
Player 2's payoff is just
the negative of that.

00:05:11.961 --> 00:05:15.240
So, if Player 2
guesses correctly,

00:05:15.241 --> 00:05:17.080
Player 1 gets minus one,

00:05:17.081 --> 00:05:20.240
and if Player 2 guesses
incorrectly or forfeits,

00:05:20.241 --> 00:05:21.555
Player 1 gets one.

00:05:21.556 --> 00:05:27.560
Okay? Now, unlike
Perfect-information games,

00:05:27.561 --> 00:05:28.850
there's this extra structure

00:05:28.851 --> 00:05:30.450
here called the information set.

00:05:30.451 --> 00:05:32.810
Often times noted
by this dashed line

00:05:32.811 --> 00:05:35.740
or this purple ellipse.

00:05:35.741 --> 00:05:38.670
That just represents
a fact that when

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:41.790
Player 2 has his turn to move,

00:05:41.791 --> 00:05:43.100
he doesn't know whether he's in

00:05:43.101 --> 00:05:45.550
the left blue node or
the right blue node.

00:05:45.551 --> 00:05:47.180
He has to infer

00:05:47.181 --> 00:05:50.630
that information from
Player 1's strategy,

00:05:50.631 --> 00:05:53.080
which Player 2 doesn't know.

00:05:53.081 --> 00:06:02.020
Okay? So, what is
rational play here?

00:06:02.021 --> 00:06:05.910
Well, I've shown one Nash
Equilibrium of the game here.

00:06:05.911 --> 00:06:07.690
A Nash Equilibrium, in general,

00:06:07.691 --> 00:06:09.455
is a profile of strategies,

00:06:09.456 --> 00:06:11.490
one strategy for each player,

00:06:11.491 --> 00:06:13.670
where no player can improve

00:06:13.671 --> 00:06:15.380
their expected utility by

00:06:15.381 --> 00:06:17.310
deviating to
a different strategy.

00:06:17.311 --> 00:06:19.880
What's a strategy? Well,
it's just a mapping from

00:06:19.881 --> 00:06:22.310
information sets to
action probabilities

00:06:22.311 --> 00:06:24.305
at that information set.

00:06:24.306 --> 00:06:28.100
An Epsilon Nash Equilibrium is

00:06:28.101 --> 00:06:30.230
a strategy profile
where no player can

00:06:30.231 --> 00:06:33.615
improve more than
epsilon, in expectation.

00:06:33.616 --> 00:06:36.290
Note that in Nash
Equilibrium the beliefs are

00:06:36.291 --> 00:06:39.860
derived on the fly as
we go down the game,

00:06:39.861 --> 00:06:41.900
from the equilibrium, and the

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:44.510
observation so far
using Bayes rule.

00:06:44.511 --> 00:06:46.415
So, this is in some sense,

00:06:46.416 --> 00:06:49.820
game theory is a generalization
of Bayesian reasoning,

00:06:49.821 --> 00:06:52.180
and it's the generalization
of MDPs, so,

00:06:52.181 --> 00:06:53.660
this is really
a generalization of

00:06:53.661 --> 00:06:56.660
POMDPs into
a multi-agent setting.

00:06:56.661 --> 00:06:59.980
All right, so, one equilibrium
here is where Player

00:06:59.981 --> 00:07:02.705
1 goes 50-50 sell and play,

00:07:02.706 --> 00:07:03.800
by the way, here I'm now

00:07:03.801 --> 00:07:05.815
assuming the sell option
is worth zero.

00:07:05.816 --> 00:07:07.420
So you can throw away the coin,

00:07:07.421 --> 00:07:08.600
you don't get anything for it,

00:07:08.601 --> 00:07:10.950
then you don't have to
pay to get rid of it.

00:07:10.951 --> 00:07:14.480
Here 50-50, and Player 2 guesses

00:07:14.481 --> 00:07:17.265
heads half of the time and
tails half of the time.

00:07:17.266 --> 00:07:19.910
All right? This is not
the only equilibrium.

00:07:19.911 --> 00:07:21.950
Turns out that there's
an uncountable number of

00:07:21.951 --> 00:07:24.930
equilibria even in
this simple game.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:28.180
Okay. Now, to make my point,

00:07:28.181 --> 00:07:31.775
I'm going to argue that
this Subgame here,

00:07:31.776 --> 00:07:34.630
in this purple rectangle

00:07:34.631 --> 00:07:38.050
can't be solved with information
from the Subgame only.

00:07:38.051 --> 00:07:40.800
So, if this were
a complete information game,

00:07:40.801 --> 00:07:42.940
or perfect information game,
this could be.

00:07:42.941 --> 00:07:45.970
Most of the algorithms
like Minimax Search,

00:07:45.971 --> 00:07:47.600
Alpha-Beta Pruning and so on,

00:07:47.601 --> 00:07:50.600
they are based on the idea
that you can solve the Subgame

00:07:50.601 --> 00:07:52.190
and do solve the Subgame based on

00:07:52.191 --> 00:07:54.010
information only in the Subgame.

00:07:54.011 --> 00:07:55.705
This can't be done here.

00:07:55.706 --> 00:07:58.580
So, now, first, I'm
going to say that

00:07:58.581 --> 00:08:00.010
the payoffs for selling are

00:08:00.011 --> 00:08:02.805
two and minus two respectively.

00:08:02.806 --> 00:08:07.360
In that world, what
would Player 1 do?

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:11.655
&gt;&gt; Sell the heads.

00:08:11.656 --> 00:08:14.385
&gt;&gt; Sell with the heads, yes, and?

00:08:14.386 --> 00:08:15.555
&gt;&gt; Play with the tails.

00:08:15.556 --> 00:08:17.310
&gt;&gt; Play with tails, that's right.

00:08:17.311 --> 00:08:19.725
So, when Player 2 gets a move,

00:08:19.726 --> 00:08:21.985
Player 2 knows that it's tails.

00:08:21.986 --> 00:08:23.830
So, Player 2 can get tails,

00:08:23.831 --> 00:08:27.855
it's a guess tails and win
whenever it was tails.

00:08:27.856 --> 00:08:30.440
Now, if I flip the payoffs
in these triangles

00:08:30.441 --> 00:08:32.860
now, you'd get the opposite,

00:08:32.861 --> 00:08:38.880
and Player 2 should guess
heads whenever he gets a turn.

00:08:38.881 --> 00:08:42.080
So, that just proves
that how Player 2 should

00:08:42.081 --> 00:08:44.255
play depends on information

00:08:44.256 --> 00:08:45.990
that's outside of the Subgame,

00:08:45.991 --> 00:08:47.230
namely in these triangles,

00:08:47.231 --> 00:08:48.770
and here it's a simple example,

00:08:48.771 --> 00:08:51.170
those triangles could
be arbitrarily big and

00:08:51.171 --> 00:08:55.250
have transfer effects in the
game from very far away.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.990
Try to remember this game
because I'm going to

00:08:57.991 --> 00:09:00.235
use this as a running
example later on.

00:09:00.236 --> 00:09:02.840
Any questions from
the game itself?

00:09:02.841 --> 00:09:07.845
Okay. So, when we tackle
imperfect-information games,

00:09:07.846 --> 00:09:11.020
we can't use techniques for
perfect-information games.

00:09:11.021 --> 00:09:14.030
We develop application-independent
techniques.

00:09:14.031 --> 00:09:16.900
So, these are not
poker techniques, and also,

00:09:16.901 --> 00:09:20.370
they're algorithmically
creating the strategies.

00:09:20.371 --> 00:09:21.500
So, we're not programming

00:09:21.501 --> 00:09:23.800
the strategies by hand
or anything like that.

00:09:23.801 --> 00:09:25.940
Challenges here,
well, uncertainty

00:09:25.941 --> 00:09:27.955
about what others
and chance will do.

00:09:27.956 --> 00:09:29.490
But more importantly,

00:09:29.491 --> 00:09:31.020
uncertainty about what others

00:09:31.021 --> 00:09:32.565
and chance have already done.

00:09:32.566 --> 00:09:34.985
That gives me hidden state.

00:09:34.986 --> 00:09:38.655
Then I have to think
about what do my actions

00:09:38.656 --> 00:09:42.730
signal to my opponents about
my private information?

00:09:42.731 --> 00:09:45.420
What do my opponents'
actions signal to

00:09:45.421 --> 00:09:48.280
me about the opponents'
private information?

00:09:48.281 --> 00:09:50.760
The really cool thing
here is that we don't

00:09:50.761 --> 00:09:53.170
have to assume a meaning
for the signals.

00:09:53.171 --> 00:09:55.480
That automatically falls out of

00:09:55.481 --> 00:09:58.420
the classic solution concepts
in game theory,

00:09:58.421 --> 00:10:01.265
like Nash equilibrium
and it's refinements.

00:10:01.266 --> 00:10:04.020
So, the real work
here is to marry

00:10:04.021 --> 00:10:07.950
those classical solution
concepts with algorithms so

00:10:07.951 --> 00:10:13.070
we can compute something that's
near a Nash Equilibrium.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:16.320
So poker itself, that has been

00:10:16.321 --> 00:10:18.780
a recognized challenge problem
in game theory, Or,

00:10:18.781 --> 00:10:20.580
and AI for decades,

00:10:20.581 --> 00:10:24.205
and if you actually go back
to John Nash's PhD thesis,

00:10:24.206 --> 00:10:26.445
which actually look very
short and you showed,

00:10:26.446 --> 00:10:28.530
where he introduced
Nash equilibrium,

00:10:28.531 --> 00:10:29.820
which really transformed all of

00:10:29.821 --> 00:10:33.610
economics and
many other sciences,

00:10:33.611 --> 00:10:37.470
the one and only example
there was poker.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:41.360
But that was a poker
game called Kuhn poker,

00:10:41.361 --> 00:10:43.730
which has three cards in

00:10:43.731 --> 00:10:44.960
the deck and only one

00:10:44.961 --> 00:10:47.200
card in each of the
two players hands.

00:10:47.201 --> 00:10:49.145
So, it's a very
small form of poker.

00:10:49.146 --> 00:10:50.500
So small that you can actually

00:10:50.501 --> 00:10:52.700
solve it by hand, paper, and pen.

00:10:52.701 --> 00:10:56.090
Then, there's been steady
progress in poker,

00:10:56.091 --> 00:10:59.635
but really there's been
tremendous explosion in

00:10:59.636 --> 00:11:01.350
work on using poker as

00:11:01.351 --> 00:11:05.000
a benchmark over
the last 14 years.

00:11:05.001 --> 00:11:07.175
Hundreds of papers,
I'm not going to

00:11:07.176 --> 00:11:09.175
try to do a full
literature review.

00:11:09.176 --> 00:11:12.085
I'm going to mention
three highlights here.

00:11:12.086 --> 00:11:14.315
One is Rhode Island Hold'em.

00:11:14.316 --> 00:11:16.120
This is a game that
has three billion

00:11:16.121 --> 00:11:17.645
nodes in the game tree,

00:11:17.646 --> 00:11:21.580
and we were able to solve
it exactly in 2005.

00:11:21.581 --> 00:11:23.760
So, this was about
four or five orders of

00:11:23.761 --> 00:11:26.425
magnitude bigger than what
had been solved before.

00:11:26.426 --> 00:11:30.350
The key idea here was
lossless abstraction.

00:11:30.351 --> 00:11:31.660
So, you can actually

00:11:31.661 --> 00:11:33.780
algorithmically walk
through the game tree,

00:11:33.781 --> 00:11:36.810
get rid of 99 percent
of it roughly,

00:11:36.811 --> 00:11:40.735
and you get a game,

00:11:40.736 --> 00:11:42.330
which you solve, and then you get

00:11:42.331 --> 00:11:44.240
an exact answer to
the original game.

00:11:44.241 --> 00:11:46.940
So, in some sense, there are

00:11:46.941 --> 00:11:50.625
so many weird isomorphisms
in these games,

00:11:50.626 --> 00:11:51.830
but you can get rid of all of

00:11:51.831 --> 00:11:54.155
them and still get
an exact solution,

00:11:54.156 --> 00:11:55.560
although you're
solving a game that's

00:11:55.561 --> 00:11:58.180
only one-one-hundredth
of the size.

00:11:58.181 --> 00:12:02.580
Then, with Simplex's
LP solver in 25 days,

00:12:02.581 --> 00:12:03.680
in a small supercomputer,

00:12:03.681 --> 00:12:05.120
we were able to solve that

00:12:05.121 --> 00:12:07.625
abstracted game to get an answer.

00:12:07.626 --> 00:12:10.240
Then the Annual Computer
Poker Competition

00:12:10.241 --> 00:12:12.375
started in 2006,

00:12:12.376 --> 00:12:14.850
where different teams
around the world

00:12:14.851 --> 00:12:17.180
compete on the same benchmarks,

00:12:17.181 --> 00:12:18.410
and you can measure progress year

00:12:18.411 --> 00:12:19.825
to year and group to group,

00:12:19.826 --> 00:12:23.890
and that has really fueled
progress in the field

00:12:23.891 --> 00:12:25.910
because most of the top teams
have been publishing

00:12:25.911 --> 00:12:28.540
their results and building
on each other's work.

00:12:28.541 --> 00:12:31.830
Then, Limit Texas Hold'em
was near optimally

00:12:31.831 --> 00:12:34.805
solved by boiling it all in 2015.

00:12:34.806 --> 00:12:36.605
That's another science paper,

00:12:36.606 --> 00:12:38.970
and that's a game that has,

00:12:38.971 --> 00:12:40.955
after lossless abstraction,

00:12:40.956 --> 00:12:43.375
10 to the 13 information sets,

00:12:43.376 --> 00:12:46.260
10 to the 13 decision points.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:50.860
This was the heads-up variant,
which is two-player,

00:12:50.861 --> 00:12:53.910
and limit means that whenever
it's your turn to move,

00:12:53.911 --> 00:12:56.880
if you bet, there's
a fixed amount you can bet.

00:12:56.881 --> 00:12:59.350
There's no choice as
to how much you bet,

00:12:59.351 --> 00:13:01.500
and that means that
the branching factor is two or

00:13:01.501 --> 00:13:04.410
three depending on the situation.

00:13:04.411 --> 00:13:07.330
Now, Heads-up No-Limit Texas

00:13:07.331 --> 00:13:09.310
Hold'em is a whole other animal.

00:13:09.311 --> 00:13:12.020
This has become the main
benchmark and challenge problem

00:13:12.021 --> 00:13:15.085
in AI for imperfect
information game solving.

00:13:15.086 --> 00:13:17.060
It has 10 to the power of

00:13:17.061 --> 00:13:20.120
161 different situations a player

00:13:20.121 --> 00:13:22.990
can face, information sets.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.270
For this audience, I
probably don't need to

00:13:26.271 --> 00:13:27.840
explain what that number means,

00:13:27.841 --> 00:13:31.175
but it's more than the number
of atoms in the universe,

00:13:31.176 --> 00:13:32.640
and even if you had a whole other

00:13:32.641 --> 00:13:33.950
universe for every atom in

00:13:33.951 --> 00:13:36.840
the universe and counted
those, it's more than that.

00:13:36.841 --> 00:13:39.335
So, you're not about to
store this anywhere,

00:13:39.336 --> 00:13:40.790
and you're not about to traverse

00:13:40.791 --> 00:13:42.535
this game tree even once.

00:13:42.536 --> 00:13:45.610
So, you need some totally
different techniques.

00:13:45.611 --> 00:13:48.060
This is mostly played
on the Internet,

00:13:48.061 --> 00:13:52.200
but it's also played on TV
and in the top poker movies.

00:13:52.201 --> 00:13:54.390
Many people consider
this a purest

00:13:54.391 --> 00:13:56.440
form of poker because
it's really two players,

00:13:56.441 --> 00:13:58.035
so there are no third players

00:13:58.036 --> 00:13:59.820
or fourth players that
they're going to mess it up.

00:13:59.821 --> 00:14:02.370
It's really am I better
or is Max better?

00:14:02.371 --> 00:14:05.860
Just like chess. No prior AI

00:14:05.861 --> 00:14:08.180
has beaten top
humans at this game.

00:14:08.181 --> 00:14:13.000
All right. So, how many of
you actually know this game?

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:15.270
Okay. So, not everybody.

00:14:15.271 --> 00:14:16.330
So, I'm going to walk through

00:14:16.331 --> 00:14:17.980
this a little bit
to the extent you

00:14:17.981 --> 00:14:21.010
need to know how the rules
work. Just on one slide.

00:14:21.011 --> 00:14:23.350
So, first, Chance
deals two cards in

00:14:23.351 --> 00:14:25.965
private to each player,
both players.

00:14:25.966 --> 00:14:27.665
Then, there's a round of betting.

00:14:27.666 --> 00:14:29.570
Then, Chance deals
three shared cards

00:14:29.571 --> 00:14:31.000
face-up on the table.

00:14:31.001 --> 00:14:33.010
Then, there's
another round of betting.

00:14:33.011 --> 00:14:36.805
Then, Chance deals one shared
card face up on the table.

00:14:36.806 --> 00:14:38.325
Then, there is
a round of betting.

00:14:38.326 --> 00:14:41.780
Then, there's a fourth round
where Chance deals

00:14:41.781 --> 00:14:43.700
one more shared card
on the table and

00:14:43.701 --> 00:14:45.700
a round of betting. All right.

00:14:45.701 --> 00:14:47.605
So, at this point, what we have,

00:14:47.606 --> 00:14:49.600
the player's holding two cards in

00:14:49.601 --> 00:14:52.350
private and five public
cards on the table.

00:14:52.351 --> 00:14:54.800
What you try to do is make

00:14:54.801 --> 00:14:58.635
the best five-card hand
out of those seven cards,

00:14:58.636 --> 00:15:00.675
and whoever has
the better five-card hand

00:15:00.676 --> 00:15:02.990
wins the whole pot.

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:05.745
The hard part is the betting.

00:15:05.746 --> 00:15:09.870
So, you have to decide
whether to bet or not,

00:15:09.871 --> 00:15:11.405
and if the opponent has bet,

00:15:11.406 --> 00:15:13.270
whether to call, fold,

00:15:13.271 --> 00:15:15.860
or bet even more, which
is called raising.

00:15:15.861 --> 00:15:19.755
If somebody folds, they
lose a pot right there.

00:15:19.756 --> 00:15:26.355
If only both players come
all the way to the end,

00:15:26.356 --> 00:15:28.095
do you actually
compare the hands?

00:15:28.096 --> 00:15:30.290
So, that's how it works.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:33.685
When I say 10 to the 161,

00:15:33.686 --> 00:15:38.450
I mean one play of the game
just as shown on the slide.

00:15:38.451 --> 00:15:42.210
So when we play, let say,
120,000 repetitions,

00:15:42.211 --> 00:15:43.880
it's not counting
the repetitions,

00:15:43.881 --> 00:15:47.785
just one hand of poker
is 10 to the 161.

00:15:47.786 --> 00:15:50.440
Any questions so far? Yes.

00:15:50.441 --> 00:15:53.665
&gt;&gt; Without us bluffing,
you factored into this.

00:15:53.666 --> 00:15:55.675
&gt;&gt; Well, let me-

00:15:55.676 --> 00:15:59.620
I will answer that at the end
if that's still unclear.

00:15:59.621 --> 00:16:04.030
Okay. So, in January 2017,

00:16:04.031 --> 00:16:07.085
I organized this Brains
versus AI Rematch,

00:16:07.086 --> 00:16:09.450
and I am calling it a
rematch because I had

00:16:09.451 --> 00:16:12.235
organized a similar match
in April 2015,

00:16:12.236 --> 00:16:13.750
where we took on four of

00:16:13.751 --> 00:16:16.420
the top humans at this game
and we couldn't beat them.

00:16:16.421 --> 00:16:19.200
It's very close.
Statistically speaking,

00:16:19.201 --> 00:16:21.190
it was actually
statistically insignificant,

00:16:21.191 --> 00:16:22.450
so a statistical type but we

00:16:22.451 --> 00:16:24.940
couldn't beat them and
they ended up ahead.

00:16:24.941 --> 00:16:28.615
So now, we have these new AI
called Libratus.

00:16:28.616 --> 00:16:31.690
It's Latin, stands for balanced,

00:16:31.691 --> 00:16:33.160
for Nash equilibrium and

00:16:33.161 --> 00:16:36.460
powerful for its forth
full play style.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:40.105
That took a while to figure
out that Latin name.

00:16:40.106 --> 00:16:42.070
Anyway, so we played
against four of

00:16:42.071 --> 00:16:44.885
the best heads of No-Limit
Texas Hold'em specialist pros.

00:16:44.886 --> 00:16:46.710
So these are
professionals not just at

00:16:46.711 --> 00:16:49.240
poker but at this
particular form of poker.

00:16:49.241 --> 00:16:51.095
It is very important,
because, you know,

00:16:51.096 --> 00:16:53.590
these guys are way stronger than

00:16:53.591 --> 00:16:56.160
a typical general TV pros
that you might

00:16:56.161 --> 00:16:59.975
see at this particular kind
of poker which is two player.

00:16:59.976 --> 00:17:05.080
We played 120,000 hands over
20 days in January 2017,

00:17:05.081 --> 00:17:07.240
so each one of them
played 30,000 hands.

00:17:07.241 --> 00:17:09.410
It is important that you

00:17:09.411 --> 00:17:11.795
play a lot of hands
for two reasons.

00:17:11.796 --> 00:17:15.955
One, you don't want this
to be decided by luck;

00:17:15.956 --> 00:17:18.865
you want it to be
statistically significant.

00:17:18.866 --> 00:17:22.350
In 2015, we played
80,000 hands and it was

00:17:22.351 --> 00:17:25.765
not enough to get 95 percent
statistical significance,

00:17:25.766 --> 00:17:27.230
which is the threshold we use in

00:17:27.231 --> 00:17:29.485
the annual computer
poker competition.

00:17:29.486 --> 00:17:32.865
So now, my worry was that if
we were a little bit behind,

00:17:32.866 --> 00:17:37.090
or even a lot behind the
humans back then, now,

00:17:37.091 --> 00:17:38.790
humans had probably
progressed a little

00:17:38.791 --> 00:17:40.680
bit but hopefully we
had progressed more,

00:17:40.681 --> 00:17:42.570
so it should have
been even closer.

00:17:42.571 --> 00:17:44.165
So, I felt that we would need

00:17:44.166 --> 00:17:47.465
even more hands to get
statistical significance.

00:17:47.466 --> 00:17:49.650
It would have been
a shame to have

00:17:49.651 --> 00:17:53.415
another event where we can't
really declare a winner.

00:17:53.416 --> 00:17:55.990
The other reason is
that you want to give

00:17:55.991 --> 00:17:58.260
the humans the best
possible chance to

00:17:58.261 --> 00:18:03.350
find weaknesses in the AI and
exploit those weaknesses.

00:18:03.351 --> 00:18:05.750
So, you may have heard about

00:18:05.751 --> 00:18:07.470
the Dota 2 bots which are able

00:18:07.471 --> 00:18:09.465
to beat top humans for a while,

00:18:09.466 --> 00:18:11.520
and then when humans get
to play with them more,

00:18:11.521 --> 00:18:13.085
even average humans
can beat them.

00:18:13.086 --> 00:18:14.990
So, you want to make sure

00:18:14.991 --> 00:18:17.015
this is not just
some sort of a transient,

00:18:17.016 --> 00:18:18.210
that for a while you can

00:18:18.211 --> 00:18:19.230
beat them when they do not really

00:18:19.231 --> 00:18:21.715
know your holes. I will
come back to that.

00:18:21.716 --> 00:18:22.225
&gt;&gt; Question.

00:18:22.226 --> 00:18:22.655
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:18:22.656 --> 00:18:25.560
&gt;&gt; How many hours are
there in playing because-?

00:18:25.561 --> 00:18:27.490
&gt;&gt; They got to choose
all the parameters.

00:18:27.491 --> 00:18:29.055
Actually, I have a whole slide,
we can talk about

00:18:29.056 --> 00:18:30.800
that in the Q&amp;A part,

00:18:30.801 --> 00:18:33.710
but they chose 20 days.

00:18:33.711 --> 00:18:36.275
We sit at poker, how many days
do you want to play over?

00:18:36.276 --> 00:18:39.040
This was a given, but
everything else they chose.

00:18:39.041 --> 00:18:41.490
How many days, when
to take breaks,

00:18:41.491 --> 00:18:44.385
break days, hours of the day;

00:18:44.386 --> 00:18:46.370
they got to choose,
even dynamically.

00:18:46.371 --> 00:18:48.360
Like on one day
they said, "Oh man,

00:18:48.361 --> 00:18:50.070
one of our players
feels really tired,

00:18:50.071 --> 00:18:53.390
maybe they are getting
a cold. Let them not play."

00:18:53.391 --> 00:18:54.970
So, we said, "Okay
yeah, this afternoon,

00:18:54.971 --> 00:18:57.810
we don't play" and so on.

00:18:57.811 --> 00:19:03.135
What it ended up being an
average maybe excluding breaks,

00:19:03.136 --> 00:19:05.005
maybe about eight hours a day.

00:19:05.006 --> 00:19:07.270
With breaks, maybe 10
hours a day,

00:19:07.271 --> 00:19:09.530
which is way less than
what they normally play.

00:19:09.531 --> 00:19:11.770
So, sometimes
these guys play like

00:19:11.771 --> 00:19:17.245
three days straight. Yeah.

00:19:17.246 --> 00:19:18.895
&gt;&gt; Did you compare

00:19:18.896 --> 00:19:20.710
the random number generator for

00:19:20.711 --> 00:19:22.990
chance maybe to get
higher statistical significance?

00:19:22.991 --> 00:19:25.530
&gt;&gt; I'll come to that. We did

00:19:25.531 --> 00:19:27.110
a conservative experiment design

00:19:27.111 --> 00:19:29.780
where we let them
pick almost all of

00:19:29.781 --> 00:19:33.075
the parameters of the tournament
so we wouldn't get into

00:19:33.076 --> 00:19:34.290
a crossfire of like

00:19:34.291 --> 00:19:37.495
whining thing at the end
in case we would win.

00:19:37.496 --> 00:19:40.170
I have a slide on
all those parameters

00:19:40.171 --> 00:19:42.660
if you are really
interested in that.

00:19:42.661 --> 00:19:45.040
&gt;&gt; Did you use the information

00:19:45.041 --> 00:19:47.905
over his gains with the pros?

00:19:47.906 --> 00:19:49.490
&gt;&gt; I'll come to that.

00:19:49.491 --> 00:19:50.220
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:19:50.221 --> 00:19:52.530
&gt;&gt; How the AI works. First,
I'll talk about the match and

00:19:52.531 --> 00:19:54.540
then I'll talk about
how the AI works.

00:19:54.541 --> 00:19:56.045
&gt;&gt; The queue of
questions hovering here.

00:19:56.046 --> 00:19:58.500
&gt;&gt; Yes. Most of
which I am skipping.

00:19:58.501 --> 00:19:59.740
It is great to take

00:19:59.741 --> 00:20:01.190
questions if you don't
have to answer them.

00:20:01.191 --> 00:20:06.350
So, this is what it looks like.

00:20:06.351 --> 00:20:09.940
So, here we have
my PhD student Noam,

00:20:09.941 --> 00:20:11.700
some of the casino personnel.

00:20:11.701 --> 00:20:15.040
Here is our dean
looking very worried.

00:20:15.041 --> 00:20:16.630
He told me up front that if you

00:20:16.631 --> 00:20:18.320
don't win this time you're fired.

00:20:18.321 --> 00:20:21.540
I hope he was joking. I
did not want to test him.

00:20:22.310 --> 00:20:24.760
Here's Jason Les one of the pros,

00:20:24.761 --> 00:20:27.425
and here's Daniel
McAulay, one of the pros.

00:20:27.426 --> 00:20:29.400
But you see only two
of the pros here.

00:20:29.401 --> 00:20:32.980
Where are the other two?
So, this is a casino floor.

00:20:32.981 --> 00:20:34.830
These guys are playing in public,

00:20:34.831 --> 00:20:36.210
the other two are playing in

00:20:36.211 --> 00:20:39.135
a private isolation chamber
on the second floor,

00:20:39.136 --> 00:20:41.330
where per your question,

00:20:41.331 --> 00:20:43.260
we are actually doing
duplicate matches.

00:20:43.261 --> 00:20:46.315
So whatever hand Daniel
gets against the AI,

00:20:46.316 --> 00:20:50.180
his paired human in that match,

00:20:50.181 --> 00:20:52.790
the AI gets that
against the human.

00:20:52.791 --> 00:20:55.350
So, neither the humans
nor the AI can

00:20:55.351 --> 00:20:57.060
systematically get lucky because

00:20:57.061 --> 00:20:59.710
they are essentially
getting the same hands.

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:03.240
Of course, you have to
keep them in isolation;

00:21:03.241 --> 00:21:04.380
otherwise, they could
tell each other

00:21:04.381 --> 00:21:06.600
what hands are coming next.

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:10.795
All right. So, we are
having some fun with Jason.

00:21:10.796 --> 00:21:12.105
This is what it looks like.

00:21:12.106 --> 00:21:15.545
They actually chose to
play two tables at once.

00:21:15.546 --> 00:21:17.490
So a left table, right table

00:21:17.491 --> 00:21:20.255
and these are different hands,

00:21:20.256 --> 00:21:22.860
and they can choose to
play like one, one, one,

00:21:22.861 --> 00:21:24.025
one or play all of

00:21:24.026 --> 00:21:26.970
the hands of a session
here, and then here,

00:21:26.971 --> 00:21:28.480
but they like to do
this because they're

00:21:28.481 --> 00:21:30.295
used to playing multi-table,

00:21:30.296 --> 00:21:33.030
and they can then let

00:21:33.031 --> 00:21:34.550
their brains to one some of

00:21:34.551 --> 00:21:36.165
the tough questions in one table,

00:21:36.166 --> 00:21:38.190
while making progress
on the other table.

00:21:38.191 --> 00:21:42.200
So, this is one of the
parameters that they selected.

00:21:42.201 --> 00:21:45.970
Here you have a chat window

00:21:45.971 --> 00:21:47.155
which they could choose

00:21:47.156 --> 00:21:49.350
individually whether
to keep on or not.

00:21:49.351 --> 00:21:51.120
So, whether the audience could

00:21:51.121 --> 00:21:53.430
actually interact with
them, and Jason loved it,

00:21:53.431 --> 00:21:55.370
he felt that he plays
best when the audience

00:21:55.371 --> 00:21:57.955
is cheering him on,
and for mankind.

00:21:57.956 --> 00:22:01.420
Some of the other humans
felt that "No,

00:22:01.421 --> 00:22:02.550
no, I am going to turn it off.

00:22:02.551 --> 00:22:03.680
I want to just focus and I am

00:22:03.681 --> 00:22:05.060
best when I am just focused."

00:22:05.061 --> 00:22:07.100
&gt;&gt; Peoples do and I've questions

00:22:07.101 --> 00:22:09.230
and they take your time to
share, they go back and forth.

00:22:09.231 --> 00:22:09.920
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:09.921 --> 00:22:16.320
&gt;&gt; Is there a called
responsiveness people have,

00:22:16.321 --> 00:22:18.690
whether they actually
stew for a while.

00:22:18.691 --> 00:22:21.460
&gt;&gt; Depends on the hand.
So, most times

00:22:21.461 --> 00:22:22.665
are very easy to play.

00:22:22.666 --> 00:22:24.200
So, most times they

00:22:24.201 --> 00:22:26.470
just know how to play
optimally very quickly,

00:22:26.471 --> 00:22:27.630
and then there are
some difficult times

00:22:27.631 --> 00:22:28.825
where they take more time.

00:22:28.826 --> 00:22:31.210
We had no time limit
for the humans.

00:22:31.211 --> 00:22:35.950
So, actually, the timing
was interesting.

00:22:35.951 --> 00:22:40.485
We got a lot of angry comments
on the chat saying,

00:22:40.486 --> 00:22:42.520
"Oh, the bot thinks too long."

00:22:42.521 --> 00:22:44.800
Well, it turns out

00:22:44.801 --> 00:22:47.680
that on average the humans
thought 20 seconds,

00:22:47.681 --> 00:22:49.700
the bot thought 13.

00:22:49.701 --> 00:22:52.345
So, the bot was actually
playing faster than the humans.

00:22:52.346 --> 00:22:57.400
But its thinking behavior
was very different.

00:22:57.401 --> 00:22:59.960
So, first of all,
the AI didn't think at

00:22:59.961 --> 00:23:02.295
all in the first two
betting rounds.

00:23:02.296 --> 00:23:04.125
It's basically using
a lookup table,

00:23:04.126 --> 00:23:05.500
and then it would tank.

00:23:05.501 --> 00:23:06.630
It will stop to think for

00:23:06.631 --> 00:23:07.900
30 or 45 seconds in

00:23:07.901 --> 00:23:09.585
the beginning of
the third betting round,

00:23:09.586 --> 00:23:11.780
which is a very unusual timing
for the humans,

00:23:11.781 --> 00:23:14.580
so they got
disoriented with that,

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:18.045
I mean the audience.

00:23:18.046 --> 00:23:21.480
The other thing is
that humans generally

00:23:21.481 --> 00:23:25.130
think harder when
there's a lot at stake.

00:23:25.131 --> 00:23:27.355
So, if there's a lot
of money in the pot,

00:23:27.356 --> 00:23:29.460
the humans think harder

00:23:29.461 --> 00:23:31.300
than if there's
a little bit of money.

00:23:31.301 --> 00:23:33.365
The AI does the exact opposite.

00:23:33.366 --> 00:23:35.635
Because the less money
there is in the pot,

00:23:35.636 --> 00:23:37.970
the more re-raise
possibilities there are,

00:23:37.971 --> 00:23:40.850
and the bigger the gain tree
after that base.

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:44.360
So, the AI would
actually think the

00:23:44.361 --> 00:23:46.995
longest when there was
the least at stake,

00:23:46.996 --> 00:23:48.790
which was weird for
the audience as well.

00:23:48.791 --> 00:23:52.400
Yeah.

00:23:52.401 --> 00:23:54.335
&gt;&gt; So, almost the game work

00:23:54.336 --> 00:23:57.800
assumes that humans are
perfectly rational,

00:23:57.801 --> 00:23:59.980
they are as powerful as

00:23:59.981 --> 00:24:03.370
the machine in terms of
solving these trees.

00:24:03.371 --> 00:24:06.400
Do you think these guys
are closer to

00:24:06.401 --> 00:24:07.600
those assumptions and what

00:24:07.601 --> 00:24:09.520
happens if those assumptions
are not [inaudible]?

00:24:09.521 --> 00:24:12.160
&gt;&gt; Good, good, good. So, what

00:24:12.161 --> 00:24:13.690
if the opponent isn't rational?

00:24:13.691 --> 00:24:14.880
Well, there's
a whole other line of

00:24:14.881 --> 00:24:16.490
work that has been done in

00:24:16.491 --> 00:24:17.750
the field to which we've

00:24:17.751 --> 00:24:20.150
contributed also
opponent exploitation,

00:24:20.151 --> 00:24:22.665
trying to exploit
weaknesses in the opponent.

00:24:22.666 --> 00:24:24.290
But I'm going to talk about why I

00:24:24.291 --> 00:24:26.620
decided not to turn
those things on here,

00:24:26.621 --> 00:24:31.125
because this Nash equilibrium
approach is safe.

00:24:31.126 --> 00:24:34.160
If the opponent does
something as not rational,

00:24:34.161 --> 00:24:36.070
it can actually only help me,

00:24:36.071 --> 00:24:38.335
because it's a zero-sum game.

00:24:38.336 --> 00:24:42.160
So, we wanted to build
an unbeatable AI,

00:24:42.161 --> 00:24:44.300
we didn't necessarily want to

00:24:44.301 --> 00:24:48.355
exploit the humans by
the biggest possible amount.

00:24:48.356 --> 00:24:51.275
Well, now we're going
so many questions,

00:24:51.276 --> 00:24:53.640
let me postpone and we
have a lot to cover.

00:24:53.641 --> 00:24:57.695
Okay. So, up, before we go
to the bottom line there.

00:24:57.696 --> 00:25:02.880
So, people really
believe in people.

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:07.700
So, the international
betting sites put us as

00:25:07.701 --> 00:25:10.080
a four-to-one or
five-to-one underdog

00:25:10.081 --> 00:25:12.275
on the morning of the event.

00:25:12.276 --> 00:25:16.260
So, we were walking into
this as a huge underdog.

00:25:16.261 --> 00:25:17.830
But what happened is this,

00:25:17.831 --> 00:25:20.855
Libratus beat the top humans
in this game by a lot,

00:25:20.856 --> 00:25:23.980
statistical significance at 99.98

00:25:23.981 --> 00:25:27.155
percent or a p-value of 0.0002,

00:25:27.156 --> 00:25:31.340
or if you like,
finance for Sigma.

00:25:31.341 --> 00:25:33.480
So, a very clear victory.

00:25:33.481 --> 00:25:35.990
So, my worry that
okay maybe 120,000

00:25:35.991 --> 00:25:39.090
hands wouldn't be enough
was totally unfounded.

00:25:39.091 --> 00:25:41.650
We had just blown past
the best humans in

00:25:41.651 --> 00:25:45.200
the 18 months between the
previous match and this match.

00:25:45.201 --> 00:25:50.710
The wind rate was
147 milli big blinds per game or

00:25:50.711 --> 00:25:55.660
14.7 big blinds per 100
if you're a poker player.

00:25:55.661 --> 00:25:57.900
Each of the humans
individually also lost

00:25:57.901 --> 00:26:00.535
to Libratus and this
was a big deal.

00:26:00.536 --> 00:26:04.935
So now, before we go
to the techniques.

00:26:04.936 --> 00:26:09.260
Then, I got an email from
China saying, '' It's okay.

00:26:09.261 --> 00:26:10.655
Well, that was fantastic.

00:26:10.656 --> 00:26:13.945
Now, we're in April,
2017. That was fantastic.

00:26:13.946 --> 00:26:16.715
I want you to come and do
the same thing in China.''

00:26:16.716 --> 00:26:20.200
I'm like, ''Well,
there's no value to it.

00:26:20.201 --> 00:26:22.400
We've already beaten
the best humans in the world.

00:26:22.401 --> 00:26:24.170
So, there's no scientific values.

00:26:24.171 --> 00:26:26.345
So, I don't really think so.

00:26:26.346 --> 00:26:29.480
He said, ''It's well
actually you know.''

00:26:29.481 --> 00:26:32.270
He was actually one
of our PhD graduate,

00:26:32.271 --> 00:26:37.620
a very famous Chinese PhD
graduate from the late 80s,

00:26:37.621 --> 00:26:38.845
and he said, ''Well,

00:26:38.846 --> 00:26:41.540
in China people don't know
that CMU is so great in AI.

00:26:41.541 --> 00:26:42.630
They've heard of Stanford and

00:26:42.631 --> 00:26:44.450
Harvard but not so much CMU.

00:26:44.451 --> 00:26:47.690
So, why don't you do.

00:26:47.691 --> 00:26:49.155
This is going to
be good for CMU."

00:26:49.156 --> 00:26:51.610
And I said, ''Okay, we'll
do it as an exhibition,

00:26:51.611 --> 00:26:53.280
not a scientific experiment

00:26:53.281 --> 00:26:54.790
per se. We'll do an exhibition.

00:26:54.791 --> 00:26:56.640
Because they didn't
want to play along,

00:26:56.641 --> 00:26:57.880
and my worry is that if we

00:26:57.881 --> 00:26:59.470
put some small number of hands,

00:26:59.471 --> 00:27:03.890
it's a coin flip and
that would be not good."

00:27:03.891 --> 00:27:06.170
So, we played only 36,000

00:27:06.171 --> 00:27:08.680
hands against
six Chinese poker players.

00:27:08.681 --> 00:27:10.780
By the way, the brand
that we used was

00:27:10.781 --> 00:27:13.045
not Libratus we used Lengpudashi,

00:27:13.046 --> 00:27:16.515
which was name given to
array by the Chinese team.

00:27:16.516 --> 00:27:18.825
I think it means
cold poker master

00:27:18.826 --> 00:27:23.010
and these included China's
one and only World Series of

00:27:23.011 --> 00:27:25.755
Poker Bracelet winner
and these people had

00:27:25.756 --> 00:27:27.750
expertise in computer
science and machine

00:27:27.751 --> 00:27:30.910
learning as well, not just poker.

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:37.350
When we got there, we
walked into a trap.

00:27:37.351 --> 00:27:39.690
In that, they had somehow scraped

00:27:39.691 --> 00:27:41.970
all of the hand
history of the AI.

00:27:41.971 --> 00:27:46.665
So, they had 120,000 hands
of sample of how we play,

00:27:46.666 --> 00:27:48.985
and we didn't know
this in advance.

00:27:48.986 --> 00:27:52.560
Wasn't freely explicitly
forbidden but anyway.

00:27:52.561 --> 00:27:56.340
So now, we are facing
a very different opponent.

00:27:56.341 --> 00:27:59.250
By the way, so when I got there,

00:27:59.251 --> 00:28:01.790
it's three story building
just for this event.

00:28:01.791 --> 00:28:05.670
There's this big media studio

00:28:05.671 --> 00:28:08.780
with professional poker
commutators on one floor.

00:28:08.781 --> 00:28:11.190
There's actually bent on
another floor and then this is

00:28:11.191 --> 00:28:13.330
one bottom floor where

00:28:13.331 --> 00:28:15.935
there's an elevator
shaft and a door.

00:28:15.936 --> 00:28:17.680
So, at one point, I decided

00:28:17.681 --> 00:28:18.900
that I'm going to peek in there,

00:28:18.901 --> 00:28:21.040
and guess what I saw there.

00:28:21.041 --> 00:28:24.290
A long table of analysts on

00:28:24.291 --> 00:28:26.660
their computers doing
real-time analysis

00:28:26.661 --> 00:28:28.865
of the play and giving
the humans advice.

00:28:28.866 --> 00:28:31.710
That wasn't disallowed
either but this is

00:28:31.711 --> 00:28:34.590
what we were facing. All right.

00:28:34.591 --> 00:28:41.745
So, now Lengpudashi won by
220 milli big blinds per game.

00:28:41.746 --> 00:28:44.425
So, even more than we
had one in January

00:28:44.426 --> 00:28:47.090
and it also won each of

00:28:47.091 --> 00:28:48.280
the nine sessions and beat

00:28:48.281 --> 00:28:49.930
each of the humans individually.

00:28:49.931 --> 00:28:51.840
So, what does this say?

00:28:51.841 --> 00:28:54.070
Well, first of all, it says
that the players we played

00:28:54.071 --> 00:28:56.310
in January were really good,

00:28:56.311 --> 00:28:58.790
and that they played
better even without

00:28:58.791 --> 00:29:02.000
any background history
on how we play.

00:29:02.001 --> 00:29:05.980
But the other thing is
really more fundamental.

00:29:05.981 --> 00:29:07.460
It's really showing the power of

00:29:07.461 --> 00:29:11.110
game theory as opposed to
say reinforcement learning.

00:29:11.111 --> 00:29:14.700
In that, the game
theory approach is

00:29:14.701 --> 00:29:18.950
thinking about all possible
strategies of the opponent.

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:23.240
So, it doesn't matter that
they know our strategy.

00:29:23.241 --> 00:29:28.260
We're basically not exactly
Nash, but approximately Nash.

00:29:28.261 --> 00:29:30.115
But an exact Nash
equilibrium would be

00:29:30.116 --> 00:29:33.485
unbeatable even if they
know exactly our strategy.

00:29:33.486 --> 00:29:37.120
This is unlike what has
been seen in RL approaches.

00:29:37.121 --> 00:29:38.660
It's like Go and Dota 2.

00:29:38.661 --> 00:29:41.070
So, if you've seen
the Go documentary,

00:29:41.071 --> 00:29:43.920
even the European champion
who wasn't really the best in

00:29:43.921 --> 00:29:45.600
the world was able to beat

00:29:45.601 --> 00:29:48.060
AlphaGo after some
practice with it.

00:29:48.061 --> 00:29:50.430
In Dota 2, even
average humans can

00:29:50.431 --> 00:29:53.165
beat it once they get
some practice with it.

00:29:53.166 --> 00:29:57.830
So, this is a big lesson,
I think. All right.

00:29:57.831 --> 00:29:59.750
Maybe there's something
more general in this

00:29:59.751 --> 00:30:01.530
as well to go on
a limb a little bit,

00:30:01.531 --> 00:30:04.195
maybe for adversarial
machine learning.

00:30:04.196 --> 00:30:05.890
maybe this will be some
more game theoretic

00:30:05.891 --> 00:30:07.520
thinking, and so on.

00:30:07.521 --> 00:30:09.745
I know there's some initial
connections already.

00:30:09.746 --> 00:30:11.600
So, how does Libratus work?

00:30:11.601 --> 00:30:12.950
Well, the high level point

00:30:12.951 --> 00:30:14.730
is provably correct techniques,

00:30:14.731 --> 00:30:16.275
so you won't see,

00:30:16.276 --> 00:30:18.865
for example, deep learning here.

00:30:18.866 --> 00:30:22.100
These are all theoretically
correct techniques.

00:30:22.101 --> 00:30:24.670
We were running on Bridges,

00:30:24.671 --> 00:30:26.100
which is a newly supercomputer

00:30:26.101 --> 00:30:28.300
at the Pittsburgh
supercomputing center.

00:30:28.301 --> 00:30:30.655
We were running on
CPUs back then.

00:30:30.656 --> 00:30:33.030
We also have GPU codes now.

00:30:33.031 --> 00:30:35.260
In the end, time
allowing I'll talk

00:30:35.261 --> 00:30:37.255
about something that can
be done on one server,

00:30:37.256 --> 00:30:38.610
which is not quite as good,

00:30:38.611 --> 00:30:40.680
but the newer technique.

00:30:40.681 --> 00:30:44.455
So, the architecture has
really three main pieces.

00:30:44.456 --> 00:30:46.735
One is the pre-computation over

00:30:46.736 --> 00:30:49.830
what we call a blueprint
strategy for the whole game.

00:30:49.831 --> 00:30:53.125
It takes on as input
rules of that game,

00:30:53.126 --> 00:30:55.020
runs an abstraction algorithm,

00:30:55.021 --> 00:30:57.700
and then runs an approximate
equilibrium finding

00:30:57.701 --> 00:31:00.370
algorithm on the abstract game to

00:31:00.371 --> 00:31:01.790
come up with a blueprint strategy

00:31:01.791 --> 00:31:04.230
for both players
for the whole game.

00:31:04.510 --> 00:31:07.365
Then there's a sub-game solver,

00:31:07.366 --> 00:31:12.470
which takes the
blueprint and solves

00:31:12.471 --> 00:31:13.740
the sub-game that's actually

00:31:13.741 --> 00:31:17.650
reached to come up with
a finer grained strategy,

00:31:17.651 --> 00:31:19.610
in a finer-grained abstraction.

00:31:19.611 --> 00:31:21.940
Now, you might said, ''Wait,
wait a second, Tuomas.

00:31:21.941 --> 00:31:23.130
You just told me in the beginning

00:31:23.131 --> 00:31:24.520
that this is impossible and

00:31:24.521 --> 00:31:25.840
now you're telling
us that you're doing

00:31:25.841 --> 00:31:27.730
it. So, what gives?''

00:31:27.731 --> 00:31:30.395
I'm going to tell you how
this is actually possible.

00:31:30.396 --> 00:31:32.370
The short answer is that
the blueprint gives

00:31:32.371 --> 00:31:33.720
enough context that you

00:31:33.721 --> 00:31:35.910
can isolate the
sub-games in there,

00:31:35.911 --> 00:31:38.200
enough that you can get

00:31:38.201 --> 00:31:40.470
provably correct
techniques even in

00:31:40.471 --> 00:31:43.115
these imperfect
information games.

00:31:43.116 --> 00:31:45.285
Then there's a
self-improvement module.

00:31:45.286 --> 00:31:47.670
Notice that this is
not the typical way

00:31:47.671 --> 00:31:49.810
of using machine
learning in games,

00:31:49.811 --> 00:31:51.585
which is opponent exploitation.

00:31:51.586 --> 00:31:53.145
Instead, I thought that,

00:31:53.146 --> 00:31:55.575
that would be very risky here.

00:31:55.576 --> 00:31:58.125
Because we're playing
against the best humans,

00:31:58.126 --> 00:32:00.920
they don't have very big
holes to exploit,

00:32:00.921 --> 00:32:03.615
and their world leading experts
at counter exploitation.

00:32:03.616 --> 00:32:06.740
So, let's not use those opponent
exploitation techniques,

00:32:06.741 --> 00:32:08.430
instead we're doing a new thing

00:32:08.431 --> 00:32:10.685
here which is that we are letting

00:32:10.686 --> 00:32:13.010
the human's actions tell us where

00:32:13.011 --> 00:32:15.710
the humans think
that we have holes,

00:32:15.711 --> 00:32:18.150
and then overnight, we are fixing

00:32:18.151 --> 00:32:19.435
those holes to play

00:32:19.436 --> 00:32:22.940
even more game theoretically
in those spots.

00:32:24.020 --> 00:32:26.340
So, that's nice in the sense

00:32:26.341 --> 00:32:27.950
that the patches
are then universal.

00:32:27.951 --> 00:32:31.285
They apply against all opponents.

00:32:31.286 --> 00:32:34.160
This is not a big data story.

00:32:34.161 --> 00:32:35.900
You could argue whether this is

00:32:35.901 --> 00:32:37.290
a machine-learning story at all,

00:32:37.291 --> 00:32:38.510
but if you think about the first

00:32:38.511 --> 00:32:39.980
two modules there's no data.

00:32:39.981 --> 00:32:41.915
It's a zero data story.

00:32:41.916 --> 00:32:46.220
You just taking on the rules
of the game as input.

00:32:47.510 --> 00:32:51.580
Here, there's minimal machine
learning as I'll show you.

00:32:51.581 --> 00:32:54.325
So, to put this in perspective,

00:32:54.326 --> 00:32:56.530
that's a boxing analog.

00:32:56.531 --> 00:32:59.975
So, it's like shadow boxing
against the mirror,

00:32:59.976 --> 00:33:01.290
and then jumping in

00:33:01.291 --> 00:33:05.030
the ring with Mike Tyson
to see what happens.

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:10.585
&gt;&gt; All right. So, now let's
go through these in order.

00:33:10.586 --> 00:33:12.790
So, the abstraction
works as follows.

00:33:12.791 --> 00:33:14.530
We have the rules of the game.

00:33:14.531 --> 00:33:16.300
The game tree is too
big to represent,

00:33:16.301 --> 00:33:18.235
so we have the rules
in compact form.

00:33:18.236 --> 00:33:20.400
We run a new
abstraction algorithm.

00:33:20.401 --> 00:33:22.400
We get an abstracted game
about 10 to the

00:33:22.401 --> 00:33:24.830
14 or 10 to the 13.

00:33:24.831 --> 00:33:25.470
Then we run

00:33:25.471 --> 00:33:27.600
a new equilibrium-finding
algorithm to come up

00:33:27.601 --> 00:33:30.360
with an epsilon equilibrium
for the abstracted game.

00:33:30.361 --> 00:33:33.025
This is about 50 terabytes
of probabilities,

00:33:33.026 --> 00:33:35.040
and then you un-abstract the

00:33:35.041 --> 00:33:39.090
answer to play the blueprint
in the original game.

00:33:39.091 --> 00:33:42.905
The abstraction has
two kinds of abstraction,

00:33:42.906 --> 00:33:45.130
one is chance abstraction
and the other

00:33:45.131 --> 00:33:48.140
is abstracting
the player's actions.

00:33:48.141 --> 00:33:52.285
Chance abstraction in poker
is abstracting the cards.

00:33:52.286 --> 00:33:55.540
We use the same algorithm
that we used in Tartanian8,

00:33:55.541 --> 00:33:58.520
which had baby version
on which won

00:33:58.521 --> 00:34:01.710
the Annual Poker
Competition in 2016.

00:34:01.711 --> 00:34:04.290
But now in a much
finer abstraction

00:34:04.291 --> 00:34:06.760
and in particular for
the first two betting rounds,

00:34:06.761 --> 00:34:07.930
we had no abstraction.

00:34:07.931 --> 00:34:09.120
So, full understanding of

00:34:09.121 --> 00:34:12.990
the cards and then in the
latter to wrap betting rounds,

00:34:12.991 --> 00:34:17.135
there's abstraction but
by the time we get there,

00:34:17.136 --> 00:34:18.785
the in-game solver kicks in,

00:34:18.786 --> 00:34:20.260
which doesn't have abstraction.

00:34:20.261 --> 00:34:21.600
So, now we never play

00:34:21.601 --> 00:34:25.820
with code abstraction at
any point in the game.

00:34:25.821 --> 00:34:28.340
Abstracting the player's actions,

00:34:28.341 --> 00:34:30.725
which is bet sizes in
the case of poker,

00:34:30.726 --> 00:34:33.100
this is largely based
on what top humans

00:34:33.101 --> 00:34:35.395
and top AIs from
the past have done,

00:34:35.396 --> 00:34:37.815
but we also added
radical bet sizes like

00:34:37.816 --> 00:34:40.990
the goal in a tiny
10 percent bets.

00:34:40.991 --> 00:34:42.670
Why? Well, we just wanted

00:34:42.671 --> 00:34:44.090
to see whether it will ever play

00:34:44.091 --> 00:34:45.480
those with probability that's

00:34:45.481 --> 00:34:47.820
greater than zero, might as well.

00:34:47.821 --> 00:34:51.940
Then we have this first
convergent algorithm for finding

00:34:51.941 --> 00:34:55.515
optimal bet side combinations
but it's not very scalable.

00:34:55.516 --> 00:34:58.715
So, we were using that only
for the first two bet sizes.

00:34:58.716 --> 00:35:00.320
So, we were optimizing
the size of

00:35:00.321 --> 00:35:03.515
the first two bet sizes
algorithmically.

00:35:03.516 --> 00:35:07.440
Now, what's the new
equilibrium finding algorithm?

00:35:07.441 --> 00:35:08.770
Well, it's an improvement on

00:35:08.771 --> 00:35:11.630
Monte-Carlo counterfactual
regret minimization.

00:35:11.631 --> 00:35:13.790
Now on back what that means,

00:35:13.791 --> 00:35:17.320
some of you may already know
that regret minimization

00:35:17.321 --> 00:35:21.000
is this technique where you are,

00:35:21.001 --> 00:35:22.380
based on your history,
you're trying

00:35:22.381 --> 00:35:26.125
to minimize average regret.

00:35:26.126 --> 00:35:30.720
Counterfactual means
that you don't do it in

00:35:30.721 --> 00:35:32.940
the space of
strategies but you do

00:35:32.941 --> 00:35:35.475
it at every information
set separately,

00:35:35.476 --> 00:35:39.340
and you have to be careful
how to group those together.

00:35:39.341 --> 00:35:41.850
Monte-Carlo means
that you're sampling.

00:35:41.851 --> 00:35:44.320
You're not traversing
the whole game tree.

00:35:44.321 --> 00:35:47.780
Now, the new thing is
that you start visiting

00:35:47.781 --> 00:35:49.200
less often paths where

00:35:49.201 --> 00:35:51.050
our own actions don't
look promising.

00:35:51.051 --> 00:35:52.630
So, we're our own actions have

00:35:52.631 --> 00:35:56.025
very high negative
regret correctly.

00:35:56.026 --> 00:35:58.200
This gives you a speedup
because you're

00:35:58.201 --> 00:36:01.535
visiting less offering
the bad paths,

00:36:01.536 --> 00:36:03.850
so we can solve
larger abstractions.

00:36:03.851 --> 00:36:06.920
Also in imperfect-recall
abstractions,

00:36:06.921 --> 00:36:08.990
which is a state of
that abstraction,

00:36:08.991 --> 00:36:11.555
you have this issue that
many different paths

00:36:11.556 --> 00:36:14.985
lead to the same abstract
state in the future.

00:36:14.986 --> 00:36:17.680
Now, some of those paths will

00:36:17.681 --> 00:36:20.800
effectively get discounted
away with this technique.

00:36:20.801 --> 00:36:23.770
So, there's less fighting in

00:36:23.771 --> 00:36:26.400
those abstract states as

00:36:26.401 --> 00:36:28.440
to what you should do it
because for different paths,

00:36:28.441 --> 00:36:30.555
how you got there, you might
want to do different things.

00:36:30.556 --> 00:36:33.085
You get actually better
solution quality as well,

00:36:33.086 --> 00:36:36.935
not just better speed. All right.

00:36:36.936 --> 00:36:39.180
So, now let's talk about
subgame solving and this

00:36:39.181 --> 00:36:42.705
is maybe the most novel
part of the architecture.

00:36:42.706 --> 00:36:45.010
This is where we take

00:36:45.011 --> 00:36:46.680
the blueprint strategy and we

00:36:46.681 --> 00:36:48.700
compute a refined strategy in

00:36:48.701 --> 00:36:51.440
a finer-grained
abstraction in the subgame

00:36:51.441 --> 00:36:54.630
that's reached during play.

00:36:54.631 --> 00:36:56.640
If you think about
the timing of this,

00:36:56.641 --> 00:36:58.880
think about this being weeks or

00:36:58.881 --> 00:37:01.540
months of computation in
advance of the match.

00:37:01.541 --> 00:37:03.120
This like 30 or 40 seconds

00:37:03.121 --> 00:37:04.900
of computation during the hand,

00:37:04.901 --> 00:37:09.005
and this is an overnight process

00:37:09.006 --> 00:37:11.880
on let's say 200 servers.

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:14.610
Okay. Now, I'm going to jump

00:37:14.611 --> 00:37:16.390
into a little bit of detail here.

00:37:16.391 --> 00:37:17.630
So, if you don't follow,

00:37:17.631 --> 00:37:19.960
it's okay at after
five or 10 minutes,

00:37:19.961 --> 00:37:22.120
we'll get back to the high level.

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:25.640
I'm going to actually do
a transformational analogy

00:37:25.641 --> 00:37:27.130
through the history of endgame

00:37:27.131 --> 00:37:29.205
solving in imperfect
information games

00:37:29.206 --> 00:37:31.090
because there have only
been so few papers,

00:37:31.091 --> 00:37:32.470
I can actually go
through the whole thing.

00:37:32.471 --> 00:37:37.420
So, new ideas in
the subgame solver.

00:37:37.421 --> 00:37:39.800
Probably safe subgame
solving taking

00:37:39.801 --> 00:37:42.300
into account the opponent's
mistakes in the hand so far.

00:37:42.301 --> 00:37:43.780
So, let's talk about that first

00:37:43.781 --> 00:37:45.705
and there I'm going
to do the analogy.

00:37:45.706 --> 00:37:48.620
So, the first
techniques for subgame

00:37:48.621 --> 00:37:52.230
solving in imperfect
information games were unsafe.

00:37:52.231 --> 00:37:53.785
That's what we call
them nowadays.

00:37:53.786 --> 00:37:55.555
We didn't call it back then.

00:37:55.556 --> 00:37:58.770
So, let's say that this is

00:37:58.771 --> 00:38:02.210
the blueprint strategy in
this example coin toss game.

00:38:02.211 --> 00:38:06.015
It's not an exact equilibrium
or equilibrium finder.

00:38:06.016 --> 00:38:07.920
It didn't give us
an exact answers.

00:38:07.921 --> 00:38:09.510
You can see it here, we're 50-50.

00:38:09.511 --> 00:38:12.055
Here, we quarter, three-quarters.

00:38:12.056 --> 00:38:14.400
Here, there's more heads guessing

00:38:14.401 --> 00:38:16.790
and tails guessing and there's
some forfeiting going on.

00:38:16.791 --> 00:38:18.090
So, you can see that
that's not really

00:38:18.091 --> 00:38:21.550
the best strategy but
it's a decent strategy.

00:38:21.780 --> 00:38:24.515
Then what we're trying to do,

00:38:24.516 --> 00:38:26.815
we're trying to compute

00:38:26.816 --> 00:38:31.070
a better strategy for
Libratus as player two.

00:38:32.230 --> 00:38:35.360
Here, I'm going to show
all of these techniques in

00:38:35.361 --> 00:38:37.885
the same abstraction
just for simplicity.

00:38:37.886 --> 00:38:39.440
In reality, we would
want to change

00:38:39.441 --> 00:38:41.870
the abstraction so that we have

00:38:41.871 --> 00:38:43.755
a finer grain game tree here

00:38:43.756 --> 00:38:47.710
under that player
two information set.

00:38:47.711 --> 00:38:50.515
So, the original idea of

00:38:50.516 --> 00:38:53.285
unsafe subgame solving is
we just use Bayes' rule.

00:38:53.286 --> 00:38:55.060
We assume that player one plays

00:38:55.061 --> 00:38:57.400
according to these probabilities
from the blueprint.

00:38:57.401 --> 00:38:59.100
So then we get the probabilities

00:38:59.101 --> 00:39:01.570
here and here using Bayes' rule.

00:39:01.940 --> 00:39:05.695
If you compute the best
strategy to that,

00:39:05.696 --> 00:39:09.220
you'll see that player two
would always gets hit.

00:39:09.221 --> 00:39:10.980
That's a fine strategy,

00:39:10.981 --> 00:39:13.270
if player one indeed
sticks to that strategy up

00:39:13.271 --> 00:39:16.480
there but it's of course
highly exploitable in that,

00:39:16.481 --> 00:39:23.705
player one could play so that
that's always a bad guess.

00:39:23.706 --> 00:39:25.710
Okay. So, no theoretical

00:39:25.711 --> 00:39:27.690
guarantees but this just
well in practice in

00:39:27.691 --> 00:39:29.380
some domains and it seems that in

00:39:29.381 --> 00:39:31.090
bigger domains these type of

00:39:31.091 --> 00:39:35.680
problems are less prevalent
or at least less pronounced.

00:39:36.090 --> 00:39:39.970
Then the first safe subgame

00:39:39.971 --> 00:39:41.650
solving technique was called

00:39:41.651 --> 00:39:44.035
re-solve refinement
by Burch et al.

00:39:44.036 --> 00:39:47.485
Here is a little bit of
a mind-bending thing,

00:39:47.486 --> 00:39:50.150
in that we are allowing
the opponent to make

00:39:50.151 --> 00:39:54.960
an action which is
a selection of abstractions.

00:39:54.961 --> 00:39:58.835
So, here we have again
the blueprint strategy in

00:39:58.836 --> 00:40:00.910
purple and we can look

00:40:00.911 --> 00:40:03.785
at what's the expected value
of playing in here,

00:40:03.786 --> 00:40:06.330
given player two's
strategy at zero

00:40:06.331 --> 00:40:09.110
and here the expected value
of playing is half.

00:40:09.111 --> 00:40:12.060
So, now I'm going to
construct this gadget game,

00:40:12.061 --> 00:40:14.620
where I'm going to
compute the player

00:40:14.621 --> 00:40:18.290
two's strategy for the
subgame as follows.

00:40:18.291 --> 00:40:21.100
I'm going to give player
one the choice of

00:40:21.101 --> 00:40:23.940
entering the original
abstraction and taking

00:40:23.941 --> 00:40:28.460
this expected value or

00:40:28.461 --> 00:40:31.135
I'm allowing Player one
to enter the subgame,

00:40:31.136 --> 00:40:34.010
which typically would be in
a finer-grained abstraction.

00:40:34.011 --> 00:40:36.905
So again, a little bit
on mind-bending thing.

00:40:36.906 --> 00:40:38.560
Now, there's an
action that we allow

00:40:38.561 --> 00:40:41.015
the opponent to take which is
not the real world action,

00:40:41.016 --> 00:40:43.960
it's a selection
between abstractions.

00:40:45.330 --> 00:40:51.220
&gt;&gt; All right. Now, Player one

00:40:51.221 --> 00:40:53.220
can choose between entering
the subgame or taking

00:40:53.221 --> 00:40:54.520
the expected value according to

00:40:54.521 --> 00:40:56.375
the blueprint of the subgame.

00:40:56.376 --> 00:40:59.360
What we want to make sure here
in the gadget game is that

00:40:59.361 --> 00:41:01.590
the opponent's
expected value of entering

00:41:01.591 --> 00:41:05.535
the subgame is no higher
than in the blueprint.

00:41:05.536 --> 00:41:07.850
If we can guarantee that,

00:41:07.851 --> 00:41:09.430
then the strategy is probably

00:41:09.431 --> 00:41:11.270
no worse than
the blueprint strategy.

00:41:11.271 --> 00:41:12.350
In particular,

00:41:12.351 --> 00:41:15.530
if the blueprint was
Nash, this is still Nash.

00:41:16.370 --> 00:41:19.570
Any questions on that idea?

00:41:19.830 --> 00:41:25.195
Okay. Now, trust me,

00:41:25.196 --> 00:41:26.900
here's what would come out

00:41:26.901 --> 00:41:29.620
or one of the things that
could come out of your solver,

00:41:29.621 --> 00:41:31.560
these purple numbers.Then you

00:41:31.561 --> 00:41:33.570
can see that there's
the same numbers as here.

00:41:33.571 --> 00:41:35.190
So, it might actually
come up with

00:41:35.191 --> 00:41:36.945
the same strategy as a blueprint.

00:41:36.946 --> 00:41:40.140
So, what have we
actually accomplished?

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:43.900
It might miss obvious
opportunities for improvement,

00:41:43.901 --> 00:41:45.355
for example, never forfeiting.

00:41:45.356 --> 00:41:48.360
We know intuitive let
forfeiting is a bad idea.

00:41:48.361 --> 00:41:50.585
You might as well make
a guess because it's free.

00:41:50.586 --> 00:41:53.070
So, we know that these
forfeiting is a mistake,

00:41:53.071 --> 00:41:56.995
and it's still there with
positive probability.

00:41:56.996 --> 00:42:00.330
Okay. So, the next improvement to

00:42:00.331 --> 00:42:03.605
that was called Maxmargin
refinement by Moravcik et al.

00:42:03.606 --> 00:42:06.810
Here we have again, the same
idea, choice of abstraction.

00:42:06.811 --> 00:42:08.595
So, this is the same so far.

00:42:08.596 --> 00:42:09.860
It's similar to resolve,

00:42:09.861 --> 00:42:11.560
but punishes
Player one as much as

00:42:11.561 --> 00:42:15.175
possible for choosing
entering rather than out.

00:42:15.176 --> 00:42:17.300
So, we define a margin for

00:42:17.301 --> 00:42:20.305
every type that
the opponent might have.

00:42:20.306 --> 00:42:22.710
So, in auction sheet, it
would be evaluation types.

00:42:22.711 --> 00:42:24.100
Here it's heads or tails.

00:42:24.101 --> 00:42:26.020
So, for example,
the margin for heads

00:42:26.021 --> 00:42:28.320
is expected value of
the alternative of

00:42:28.321 --> 00:42:34.030
heads minus the expected value
of entering under heads.

00:42:35.380 --> 00:42:39.135
We solve this by maximizing
the minimum margin.

00:42:39.136 --> 00:42:41.070
This is in contrast
to the previous slide

00:42:41.071 --> 00:42:42.180
where we simply attend to

00:42:42.181 --> 00:42:45.580
make all of the margins
non-negative. Yes.

00:42:45.581 --> 00:42:48.280
&gt;&gt; What does entering mean like-

00:42:48.281 --> 00:42:50.170
&gt;&gt; Interested here.
You are entering

00:42:50.171 --> 00:42:53.785
the first game as opposed
to choosing the blueprint.

00:42:53.786 --> 00:42:56.080
&gt;&gt; For what do you
do after you enter?

00:42:56.081 --> 00:42:58.080
Do you get a reward
upon entering.

00:42:58.081 --> 00:43:00.810
Do you have to go all the
way to the leaf of the tree.

00:43:00.811 --> 00:43:03.060
Do you let the opponents
choose their move?

00:43:03.061 --> 00:43:07.380
&gt;&gt; We are trying to compute
a strategy for Player two.

00:43:07.381 --> 00:43:11.210
Here.

00:43:11.211 --> 00:43:13.475
In this model we are,

00:43:13.476 --> 00:43:15.150
well, let me show you that.

00:43:15.151 --> 00:43:18.130
So you will get five heads here,

00:43:18.131 --> 00:43:19.910
three heads here, zero here.

00:43:19.911 --> 00:43:21.720
So there's no
forfeiting anymore and

00:43:21.721 --> 00:43:23.010
the expected value would be

00:43:23.011 --> 00:43:25.155
minus quarter here
and a quarter here,

00:43:25.156 --> 00:43:26.715
and if you look at the margins,

00:43:26.716 --> 00:43:28.200
it's actually
balancing the margins.

00:43:28.201 --> 00:43:30.120
So the margin here is
the same as the margin

00:43:30.121 --> 00:43:32.665
here. Does it make sense now?

00:43:32.666 --> 00:43:33.540
&gt;&gt; No.

00:43:33.541 --> 00:43:34.735
&gt;&gt; No? Okay.

00:43:34.736 --> 00:43:36.600
&gt;&gt; I still don't understand
what is entering

00:43:36.601 --> 00:43:39.100
because entering is a
Player one move, right.

00:43:39.101 --> 00:43:41.690
&gt;&gt; Entering is a Player one move.

00:43:41.691 --> 00:43:43.855
That is the choice
of abstraction.

00:43:43.856 --> 00:43:47.060
Player one can take
the alternative here from

00:43:47.061 --> 00:43:48.720
the original
blueprint abstraction

00:43:48.721 --> 00:43:50.895
or into this new subgame.

00:43:50.896 --> 00:43:53.520
&gt;&gt; I see you're just
trying to maximize if

00:43:53.521 --> 00:43:56.300
he were to choose
enter, in some sense.

00:43:56.301 --> 00:43:58.740
&gt;&gt; We are trying to
maximize how much he

00:43:58.741 --> 00:44:01.485
would lose by going
enter instead of out.

00:44:01.486 --> 00:44:03.190
&gt;&gt; Yes. Okay.

00:44:03.191 --> 00:44:05.660
&gt;&gt; Yes. Here how much he would

00:44:05.661 --> 00:44:08.275
lose is a quarter on
here and a quarter here.

00:44:08.276 --> 00:44:10.720
&gt;&gt; I see so I'll
just kind of like,

00:44:10.721 --> 00:44:12.950
I don't want to play the game
anymore, let's stop here.

00:44:12.951 --> 00:44:14.265
&gt;&gt; No. It's not that.

00:44:14.266 --> 00:44:15.770
It's not that I
want to stop here.

00:44:15.771 --> 00:44:17.110
It's I want to play according

00:44:17.111 --> 00:44:18.970
to our original abstraction.

00:44:18.971 --> 00:44:22.750
&gt;&gt; Okay. All right.
That makes more sense.

00:44:22.751 --> 00:44:24.610
&gt;&gt; Let me postpone though.

00:44:24.611 --> 00:44:26.220
We're running a little late here.

00:44:26.221 --> 00:44:30.290
Okay so the newest then, well,

00:44:30.291 --> 00:44:31.410
the problem here is that while we

00:44:31.411 --> 00:44:32.590
focus on reducing Player

00:44:32.591 --> 00:44:36.045
one's expected value for
heads to minus quarter,

00:44:36.046 --> 00:44:38.110
Player one can actually
just settle for

00:44:38.111 --> 00:44:40.245
half in the hedge situation.

00:44:40.246 --> 00:44:42.950
So, he's still somehow
missing something.

00:44:42.951 --> 00:44:44.940
The newest idea is what

00:44:44.941 --> 00:44:46.920
we call Reach-Maxmargin
refinement,

00:44:46.921 --> 00:44:49.205
and now we're solving
a single sub-game first.

00:44:49.206 --> 00:44:51.560
We'll talk about
multiple in a second.

00:44:51.561 --> 00:44:54.300
Let's focus on
this yellow part here.

00:44:54.301 --> 00:44:59.375
I'm not going to show you
the gadget game but here.

00:44:59.376 --> 00:45:06.565
Somehow if Player one plays here,

00:45:06.566 --> 00:45:10.550
Player one is making
a mistake of half, right.

00:45:10.551 --> 00:45:13.310
Player one is giving
us a gift of half.

00:45:13.311 --> 00:45:15.360
So, in the gadget game we

00:45:15.361 --> 00:45:17.200
can increase the alternative
payoff following

00:45:17.201 --> 00:45:18.640
heads by half because

00:45:18.641 --> 00:45:19.770
choosing play would still be

00:45:19.771 --> 00:45:21.180
a mistake for Player one there.

00:45:21.181 --> 00:45:23.370
So we can afford to
give half of gifts,

00:45:23.371 --> 00:45:25.980
half which is
the size of the gift

00:45:25.981 --> 00:45:29.860
back and it will still be
a mistake or half minus X1.

00:45:29.960 --> 00:45:33.440
That's the gadget game solver
focuses on reducing Player

00:45:33.441 --> 00:45:36.345
one's expected value for
other type she may have.

00:45:36.346 --> 00:45:38.620
So that's the only
extra thing over

00:45:38.621 --> 00:45:40.880
the previous slide is that
we're keeping track of

00:45:40.881 --> 00:45:42.940
these gifts and we can afford for

00:45:42.941 --> 00:45:46.210
some types to give
back something,

00:45:46.211 --> 00:45:48.220
and those are exactly
the types where the gift

00:45:48.221 --> 00:45:50.110
was positive for
others it was zero,

00:45:50.111 --> 00:45:52.095
and we can't afford to
give back anything.

00:45:52.096 --> 00:45:55.290
So, this gives us in a way of
bigger polytope to optimize

00:45:55.291 --> 00:45:59.385
strategies over in
a safe way. All right.

00:45:59.386 --> 00:46:01.410
So, now two flies in
the ointment and this

00:46:01.411 --> 00:46:04.960
is kind of a side deal here,

00:46:04.961 --> 00:46:08.030
but one is that if
multiple subgames are refined

00:46:08.031 --> 00:46:11.340
the off path expected values
might not remain constant.

00:46:11.341 --> 00:46:19.335
So, here let's say we have
this thing gift of half here.

00:46:19.336 --> 00:46:24.830
Now, if we change,

00:46:24.831 --> 00:46:25.975
take the gift here,

00:46:25.976 --> 00:46:28.910
say that that's one.
This is half now.

00:46:28.911 --> 00:46:30.315
We can also take,

00:46:30.316 --> 00:46:32.140
or we can take the
gift here but we can't

00:46:32.141 --> 00:46:33.850
take the gift on both sides or

00:46:33.851 --> 00:46:35.460
different subgame themselves in

00:46:35.461 --> 00:46:37.135
different hands because now

00:46:37.136 --> 00:46:39.280
we've made ourselves exploitable.

00:46:39.281 --> 00:46:41.730
Solution to this is we
split the gifts among

00:46:41.731 --> 00:46:44.740
the subgames by the probability
that subgame is reached.

00:46:44.741 --> 00:46:46.415
So, for example here,

00:46:46.416 --> 00:46:48.770
if this is half half
we take half of

00:46:48.771 --> 00:46:51.095
the gift on this side and half
of the gift on this side.

00:46:51.096 --> 00:46:52.490
There are other ways
that are safe

00:46:52.491 --> 00:46:53.980
as well but you can't count

00:46:53.981 --> 00:46:55.295
the full gift on

00:46:55.296 --> 00:46:58.840
all sides because now you
would no longer be safe.

00:46:59.190 --> 00:47:01.630
Other fly in the ointment

00:47:01.631 --> 00:47:03.250
is that the gifts might
not be as large as

00:47:03.251 --> 00:47:04.680
we thought because the subgames

00:47:04.681 --> 00:47:06.505
they come from and
will be improved.

00:47:06.506 --> 00:47:08.475
So, let's say here,

00:47:08.476 --> 00:47:10.570
we thought that gift is half here

00:47:10.571 --> 00:47:12.780
but because we're going
to improve the subgame,

00:47:12.781 --> 00:47:15.100
the actual gift is only a quarter

00:47:15.101 --> 00:47:18.170
and you can substitute the
lower bound on the gift.

00:47:18.171 --> 00:47:20.605
It can even be
an estimating practice.

00:47:20.606 --> 00:47:24.135
All right, off-tree actions
and nested subgame solving.

00:47:24.136 --> 00:47:25.870
Now, this is going to
be kind of mind bending

00:47:25.871 --> 00:47:27.610
at that can actually
change the abstraction on

00:47:27.611 --> 00:47:32.075
the fly and still have
Nash Equilibrium guarantees.

00:47:32.076 --> 00:47:36.360
So, normally when the branching
factor is large in games,

00:47:36.361 --> 00:47:39.170
like in North Texas Hold'em,

00:47:39.171 --> 00:47:42.605
you actually just put some of
the actions into the model.

00:47:42.606 --> 00:47:45.220
We call this action abstraction.

00:47:45.221 --> 00:47:48.590
Then we always can play into

00:47:48.591 --> 00:47:51.420
the abstraction because
we control our actions,

00:47:51.421 --> 00:47:52.620
but the opponent might play

00:47:52.621 --> 00:47:54.295
something that's
not in our model.

00:47:54.296 --> 00:47:56.700
So, let's say the opponent
plays at green action,

00:47:56.701 --> 00:47:58.880
then we pretend that

00:47:58.881 --> 00:48:00.595
they actually played
the black action.

00:48:00.596 --> 00:48:03.875
This is called the reverse
mapping or action mapping.

00:48:03.876 --> 00:48:06.560
How do we map these green actions

00:48:06.561 --> 00:48:09.080
back into the abstraction?

00:48:09.081 --> 00:48:10.670
There's a whole literature how to

00:48:10.671 --> 00:48:12.600
do that, the newest, and latest,

00:48:12.601 --> 00:48:14.230
and greatest on
that strand is they've got

00:48:14.231 --> 00:48:17.380
13 paper and we'll
benchmark against that.

00:48:17.381 --> 00:48:20.695
Now, we want to get
rid of that entirely.

00:48:20.696 --> 00:48:26.630
So, what we do is that we
are going to solve a subgame

00:48:26.631 --> 00:48:29.165
in real time including

00:48:29.166 --> 00:48:32.235
the off-tree action that
the opponent actually took.

00:48:32.236 --> 00:48:33.980
So, now we're adding

00:48:33.981 --> 00:48:36.750
branches to our
action abstraction or

00:48:36.751 --> 00:48:38.920
a game model on
the fly and we'd still

00:48:38.921 --> 00:48:41.515
like to be able to prove
equilibrium properties.

00:48:41.516 --> 00:48:44.965
So, here's what it
would look like.

00:48:44.966 --> 00:48:47.050
So, let say Player one can take

00:48:47.051 --> 00:48:49.320
three actions with
expected values X,

00:48:49.321 --> 00:48:53.000
Y and Z, but instead
that's in our head,

00:48:53.001 --> 00:48:55.275
but in reality they
do something else.

00:48:55.276 --> 00:48:57.010
So we actually construct

00:48:57.011 --> 00:49:00.250
this same kind of
idea that Player one,

00:49:00.251 --> 00:49:03.040
the opponent, can actually
choose between abstractions,

00:49:03.041 --> 00:49:06.200
either in the original game
where he gets a maximum of X,

00:49:06.201 --> 00:49:08.775
Y and Z, the best of
the original actions,

00:49:08.776 --> 00:49:11.290
or this new action
which is generated on

00:49:11.291 --> 00:49:12.655
the fly to match

00:49:12.656 --> 00:49:16.010
exactly the action
the opponent actually took.

00:49:16.430 --> 00:49:19.305
All right. So,
properties of this.

00:49:19.306 --> 00:49:21.440
This actually works even
with hidden actions

00:49:21.441 --> 00:49:23.890
by chance which you
don't see in poker.

00:49:23.891 --> 00:49:26.135
So now let me define delta.

00:49:26.136 --> 00:49:27.380
This is not an assumption.

00:49:27.381 --> 00:49:28.775
It's a definition of delta.

00:49:28.776 --> 00:49:30.790
So, let delta be such
that there exists

00:49:30.791 --> 00:49:33.720
a Nash equilibrium where
each alt payoff for

00:49:33.721 --> 00:49:36.170
Player one is within delta of

00:49:36.171 --> 00:49:38.275
the Nash Equilibrium
expected value

00:49:38.276 --> 00:49:40.125
at that point for Player one.

00:49:40.126 --> 00:49:42.910
So, that's basically saying,

00:49:43.260 --> 00:49:45.375
well, let me leave it at that.

00:49:45.376 --> 00:49:47.230
Then our Reach-Maxmargin subway

00:49:47.231 --> 00:49:48.630
and solving technique yields

00:49:48.631 --> 00:49:50.110
a 2-delta Minimax strategy

00:49:50.111 --> 00:49:51.955
for Player two for the full game.

00:49:51.956 --> 00:49:54.810
So, this means that we
can actually change or

00:49:54.811 --> 00:49:57.570
abstraction on the fly
and we feel almost

00:49:57.571 --> 00:50:00.635
optimal and how close
to optimal we are

00:50:00.636 --> 00:50:04.345
is how off our blueprint
was from Nash.

00:50:04.346 --> 00:50:05.845
If it was an exact Nash,

00:50:05.846 --> 00:50:08.050
will still be exact Nash.

00:50:08.920 --> 00:50:12.030
Now, there's
another version of this,

00:50:12.031 --> 00:50:14.965
which talks about alt
in a different way,

00:50:14.966 --> 00:50:17.620
where if player one's alt
payoffs are computed from

00:50:17.621 --> 00:50:19.305
a best response in the full game

00:50:19.306 --> 00:50:20.935
to player two's blueprint,

00:50:20.936 --> 00:50:23.125
then the strategy formulae are

00:50:23.126 --> 00:50:25.930
reached max margin subgame
solving technique has

00:50:25.931 --> 00:50:28.190
no greater regret than
play for player two in

00:50:28.191 --> 00:50:31.245
the full game than
players' two blueprint strategy.

00:50:31.246 --> 00:50:32.770
Saying that, okay, this
is not going to make

00:50:32.771 --> 00:50:34.370
the blueprint- may make

00:50:34.371 --> 00:50:37.195
the strategy worse
than what it was.

00:50:37.196 --> 00:50:40.980
Notice that there's
no notion of Delta here,

00:50:40.981 --> 00:50:42.730
however bad your blueprint was,

00:50:42.731 --> 00:50:44.800
it's not going to get worse.

00:50:44.801 --> 00:50:48.810
Nested, these can
actually be reapplied for

00:50:48.811 --> 00:50:51.315
every subsequent opponent
of reaction as well.

00:50:51.316 --> 00:50:52.920
So, we don't have
to just do it once,

00:50:52.921 --> 00:50:54.970
we can apply the same over and

00:50:54.971 --> 00:50:56.500
over in the hand as we

00:50:56.501 --> 00:50:58.810
get deeper and deeper
into the game.

00:50:58.811 --> 00:51:01.160
Theorem one prime is

00:51:01.161 --> 00:51:03.190
that now you're not
off by two Delta,

00:51:03.191 --> 00:51:04.690
your two Delta times

00:51:04.691 --> 00:51:07.455
number of reapplications
within one play-through.

00:51:07.456 --> 00:51:12.800
So, if you're applying this
three times, it's six Delta.

00:51:13.090 --> 00:51:15.590
Theorem two prime
for this setting,

00:51:15.591 --> 00:51:17.175
well, it's the same as two.

00:51:17.176 --> 00:51:20.290
So, you're not going to
make the blueprint worse.

00:51:20.291 --> 00:51:23.150
All right. Experiments
on medium-sized games.

00:51:23.151 --> 00:51:25.895
This is not Texas Hold'em
because there you can't

00:51:25.896 --> 00:51:27.340
compute the best response to

00:51:27.341 --> 00:51:29.280
evaluate how exploitable you are.

00:51:29.281 --> 00:51:31.660
This is a medium-sized
poker game.

00:51:31.661 --> 00:51:34.305
Our best reach sub-games
solving technique,

00:51:34.306 --> 00:51:36.040
there's actually
multiple variations,

00:51:36.041 --> 00:51:38.140
has three times less
exploitability than

00:51:38.141 --> 00:51:40.290
the best prior safe
subgame solving

00:51:40.291 --> 00:51:43.340
technique and the nested
read subgame solving is 12

00:51:43.341 --> 00:51:45.020
times less exploitable and the

00:51:45.021 --> 00:51:46.980
best action mapping technique.

00:51:46.981 --> 00:51:48.695
So, indeed this idea of

00:51:48.696 --> 00:51:50.860
action mapping is dominated

00:51:50.861 --> 00:51:53.715
by this nested read
subgame solving.

00:51:53.716 --> 00:51:58.940
Okay. Also, the subgame
solving starts much earlier.

00:51:59.050 --> 00:52:02.230
Typically, had been
two years by then only.

00:52:02.231 --> 00:52:04.000
It had been on
the last betting round,

00:52:04.001 --> 00:52:05.100
now we're actually starting it

00:52:05.101 --> 00:52:06.775
already on the third
betting round.

00:52:06.776 --> 00:52:08.340
There's no card abstraction in

00:52:08.341 --> 00:52:09.920
the subgame because
we have these more

00:52:09.921 --> 00:52:12.680
scalable subgame solving
techniques and we can actually

00:52:12.681 --> 00:52:15.985
change our action
abstraction between hands.

00:52:15.986 --> 00:52:19.050
Now that we're solving
our subgame some new anyway,

00:52:19.051 --> 00:52:20.160
we might as well change

00:52:20.161 --> 00:52:21.885
our action abstraction
a little bit.

00:52:21.886 --> 00:52:23.680
Because the value
of the game is not

00:52:23.681 --> 00:52:25.960
very sensitive to the
action abstraction,

00:52:25.961 --> 00:52:28.765
but the strategy is
quite sensitive to it.

00:52:28.766 --> 00:52:31.430
This keeps our opponents
on their toes all the

00:52:31.431 --> 00:52:32.540
time because they're facing

00:52:32.541 --> 00:52:34.395
a different action
abstraction all the time,

00:52:34.396 --> 00:52:36.605
and they have to think
through it every time.

00:52:36.606 --> 00:52:41.110
Okay. So, now if
you're a poker player,

00:52:41.111 --> 00:52:42.600
you know that
these type of subgame

00:52:42.601 --> 00:52:45.390
solving would show up in
what's known as blockers.

00:52:45.391 --> 00:52:47.600
How do you think
about blocker cards?

00:52:47.601 --> 00:52:50.055
If you don't know poker,
don't worry about it.

00:52:50.056 --> 00:52:51.355
I'm going to play a video

00:52:51.356 --> 00:52:57.025
about the guy who's been
