00:53:56.416 --> 00:53:58.790
Yeah, it's a later video where

00:53:58.791 --> 00:54:01.020
we see the example. All right.

00:54:01.021 --> 00:54:02.160
So, let's talk about the last

00:54:02.161 --> 00:54:03.910
module of the self-improver.

00:54:03.911 --> 00:54:06.240
So, again, I mentioned that
we're not gonna try to

00:54:06.241 --> 00:54:08.285
do opponent exploitation here,

00:54:08.286 --> 00:54:09.930
although there are
algorithms for it.

00:54:09.931 --> 00:54:11.490
But it would be, in my opinion,

00:54:11.491 --> 00:54:13.625
it would have been too
risky, so we didn't do it.

00:54:13.626 --> 00:54:16.695
We can instead do
a different kind of idea

00:54:16.696 --> 00:54:20.195
that I think we were
the first to do.

00:54:20.196 --> 00:54:22.600
Where we let
the opponent's actions,

00:54:22.601 --> 00:54:24.490
tell us where the opponents

00:54:24.491 --> 00:54:26.710
think they've found
holes in our strategy

00:54:26.711 --> 00:54:28.300
and then we're going
to fix those holes

00:54:28.301 --> 00:54:31.855
overnight to play more game
theoretically in those spots.

00:54:31.856 --> 00:54:34.290
So, here's a simple example.

00:54:34.291 --> 00:54:36.795
Let's say Libratus moves
first, then the opponent.

00:54:36.796 --> 00:54:40.755
These are our action
abstraction in the blueprint.

00:54:40.756 --> 00:54:43.785
Let's say the opponent
has actually played

00:54:43.786 --> 00:54:46.500
also some of the actions
out of the abstraction.

00:54:46.501 --> 00:54:48.810
So, those green actions.

00:54:48.811 --> 00:54:51.020
Now, we're going to
automatically prioritize

00:54:51.021 --> 00:54:53.135
which of those are
the biggest holes.

00:54:53.136 --> 00:54:56.340
It has to do with how close you

00:54:56.341 --> 00:54:59.550
are to that prototypical actions,

00:54:59.551 --> 00:55:01.810
the blue ones, and how
often they played.

00:55:01.811 --> 00:55:03.205
The closer you are,

00:55:03.206 --> 00:55:04.605
the less of a problem it is

00:55:04.606 --> 00:55:06.120
and the more often it's played,

00:55:06.121 --> 00:55:07.500
the bigger the problem it is.

00:55:07.501 --> 00:55:09.215
So, let's say that this
is the worst problem.

00:55:09.216 --> 00:55:11.460
We call this a top one hole.

00:55:11.461 --> 00:55:13.900
Now, we're going to generate

00:55:13.901 --> 00:55:15.730
the coarser caught
abstraction for that part,

00:55:15.731 --> 00:55:18.300
so we can solve it overnight
instead of months,

00:55:18.301 --> 00:55:20.510
and we're going to
put a default action,

00:55:20.511 --> 00:55:23.265
one of the already existing blue
actions in there,

00:55:23.266 --> 00:55:25.970
in an even coarser abstraction,

00:55:25.971 --> 00:55:29.710
so that we don't end
up assuming that

00:55:29.711 --> 00:55:31.630
the opponent plays this with

00:55:31.631 --> 00:55:34.680
all possible cards that
he might be here with.

00:55:34.681 --> 00:55:36.080
Then we're going to
freeze this part

00:55:36.081 --> 00:55:37.595
of the blueprint strategy,

00:55:37.596 --> 00:55:39.090
and we're going to recompute

00:55:39.091 --> 00:55:40.845
this four Epsilon equilibrium

00:55:40.846 --> 00:55:43.715
using the same
algorithm overnight.

00:55:43.716 --> 00:55:46.110
Then we're going to
throw this one away,

00:55:46.111 --> 00:55:48.240
and this gives us a strategy

00:55:48.241 --> 00:55:49.530
for a new branch of the game tree

00:55:49.531 --> 00:55:53.115
that wasn't there. All right.

00:55:53.116 --> 00:55:54.985
We do this for the top k holes.

00:55:54.986 --> 00:55:56.560
Depending on how big your game

00:55:56.561 --> 00:55:58.475
is and how much
supercomputing you have,

00:55:58.476 --> 00:56:00.955
you can do more or less holes.

00:56:00.956 --> 00:56:02.440
We typically try to do

00:56:02.441 --> 00:56:05.140
two or three holes a night
and then in the morning

00:56:05.141 --> 00:56:07.160
we had a test against
our prior AIs

00:56:07.161 --> 00:56:09.365
to see whether the whole had
been sufficiently solved.

00:56:09.366 --> 00:56:10.770
If so, we would put it in

00:56:10.771 --> 00:56:13.935
the execution engine
and if not, then not.

00:56:13.936 --> 00:56:17.130
So now, let's see
what the humans say

00:56:17.131 --> 00:56:22.860
about the holes and the
self-fixing of the holes.

00:56:23.170 --> 00:56:26.730
&gt;&gt; The energy level is super
high the first few days.

00:56:26.731 --> 00:56:28.090
It start up in the hole,

00:56:28.091 --> 00:56:30.550
kind of expected us for
figuring out what's going on.

00:56:30.551 --> 00:56:32.210
Then we climbed all the way back

00:56:32.211 --> 00:56:34.120
to basically nearly even,

00:56:34.121 --> 00:56:35.550
and we're like, we're in this,

00:56:35.551 --> 00:56:36.980
we have a chance.

00:56:36.981 --> 00:56:39.105
We had a key strategy ideas,

00:56:39.106 --> 00:56:42.110
key things we're
pushing against the AI.

00:56:42.111 --> 00:56:47.310
Then, I guess,
the wheels just came

00:56:47.311 --> 00:56:49.720
off the wagon and it wasn't

00:56:49.721 --> 00:56:52.460
that our play fell
apart in my opinion.

00:56:52.461 --> 00:56:54.285
I think we've really seen

00:56:54.286 --> 00:56:59.920
a clear improvement in the AI
strategy day-to-day-to-day.

00:57:00.430 --> 00:57:02.930
&gt;&gt; Okay. Let's see what see

00:57:02.931 --> 00:57:05.205
what Jimmy Chou has
to say about it.

00:57:05.206 --> 00:57:08.040
&gt;&gt; The block plays incredible.

00:57:08.041 --> 00:57:10.550
The first two or three nights,

00:57:10.551 --> 00:57:14.385
I severely underestimated
its ability,

00:57:14.386 --> 00:57:16.895
I really thought that we
were just going to find

00:57:16.896 --> 00:57:19.685
a couple of simple leaks
and just hammer away at it,

00:57:19.686 --> 00:57:22.220
and will just be a easy 20,

00:57:22.221 --> 00:57:25.450
20 days, but it's been
