00:57:25.451 --> 00:57:27.075
Every time we find something,

00:57:27.076 --> 00:57:29.110
it seems to adjust
the next day and

00:57:29.111 --> 00:57:31.110
we have to continue
to look for leaks,

00:57:31.111 --> 00:57:36.830
but eventually, that we just
were not able find any.

00:57:36.831 --> 00:57:41.285
&gt;&gt; Okay. Head-to-head strength
of the top AIs.

00:57:41.286 --> 00:57:44.240
So, higher on
the slide is stronger.

00:57:44.241 --> 00:57:46.570
Here are ours on the left,
others on the right.

00:57:46.571 --> 00:57:47.720
There are many other AIs, these

00:57:47.721 --> 00:57:49.140
are just showing the top ones.

00:57:49.141 --> 00:57:52.385
I guess the bottom line
here is that

00:57:52.386 --> 00:57:54.350
usually you see an improvement of

00:57:54.351 --> 00:57:57.580
maybe 10 or 20 milli big blinds
per game per year,

00:57:57.581 --> 00:58:01.160
and here we saw in 18 months
we saw 63 milli big blinds,

00:58:01.161 --> 00:58:02.350
which is a big improvement

00:58:02.351 --> 00:58:04.670
mostly driven by the
subgame solving.

00:58:04.671 --> 00:58:06.170
It didn't even measure

00:58:06.171 --> 00:58:09.160
the contribution of
the last component.

00:58:09.161 --> 00:58:11.270
Another kind of interesting thing

00:58:11.271 --> 00:58:13.010
is that, this Modicum AI,

00:58:13.011 --> 00:58:14.815
which is our newest AI,

00:58:14.816 --> 00:58:16.230
which is not
the strongest Libratus,

00:58:16.231 --> 00:58:19.020
but this was actually
built just on one server.

00:58:19.021 --> 00:58:22.330
So, this is not a supercomputing
deal here at all.

00:58:22.390 --> 00:58:25.875
So, observations
about Libratus' play.

00:58:25.876 --> 00:58:29.835
Well, strengths, it plays
very in an unusual way.

00:58:29.836 --> 00:58:34.315
So, if you're looking for
heterogeneity in AIs for games,

00:58:34.316 --> 00:58:38.310
this is what you- it's
better to actually have at

00:58:38.311 --> 00:58:39.540
least one of the things

00:58:39.541 --> 00:58:42.270
be something I've
generated from scratch,

00:58:42.271 --> 00:58:44.050
rather than copying
different humans.

00:58:44.051 --> 00:58:46.300
Because humans always
play in a subspace

00:58:46.301 --> 00:58:48.885
of the game strategy space.

00:58:48.886 --> 00:58:51.620
Small bets and huge bets
and huge all-ins

00:58:51.621 --> 00:58:53.000
plays a totally different kind of

00:58:53.001 --> 00:58:55.300
poker around on humans
had been used to.

00:58:55.301 --> 00:58:58.340
Multiple bet sizes in
any one situation,

00:58:58.341 --> 00:59:01.235
he must typically play
one or at most two.

00:59:01.236 --> 00:59:04.240
It places actions called
limping and donk betting,

00:59:04.241 --> 00:59:06.945
which poker books
universally say are bad.

00:59:06.946 --> 00:59:08.505
We do them regularly.

00:59:08.506 --> 00:59:11.175
Not all the time, but we do
them with good probability.

00:59:11.176 --> 00:59:12.950
In all of the AIs, for example,

00:59:12.951 --> 00:59:14.340
that we've generated, limping

00:59:14.341 --> 00:59:16.600
happens with eight to 12 percent.

00:59:16.601 --> 00:59:20.110
So, that suggests that it's
not really a bad action.

00:59:20.111 --> 00:59:21.830
Perfect balance.

00:59:21.831 --> 00:59:23.170
While Nash would've been perfect,

00:59:23.171 --> 00:59:24.830
of course we're not exactly Nash.

00:59:24.831 --> 00:59:27.155
But that's what the
humans call it.

00:59:27.156 --> 00:59:29.930
Mixed strategy, probability

00:59:29.931 --> 00:59:31.605
distributions over
player's hands,

00:59:31.606 --> 00:59:33.370
not just range-based thinking,

00:59:33.371 --> 00:59:34.810
which is really the supports of

