01:02:28.076 --> 01:02:30.120
So, now, let me pop up back to

01:02:30.121 --> 01:02:31.650
the higher level of
some current research

01:02:31.651 --> 01:02:32.670
on this topic in my lab,

01:02:32.671 --> 01:02:34.935
and I mean this topic,
as in game solving.

01:02:34.936 --> 01:02:37.305
We're doing other things
like kidney exchange,

01:02:37.306 --> 01:02:39.910
advertising in markets and
things like that as well,

01:02:39.911 --> 01:02:42.360
but now I'll just focus on this.

01:02:42.361 --> 01:02:46.325
So, one is lossy abstraction
algorithms with bounds.

01:02:46.326 --> 01:02:48.130
So, the abstraction algorithms

01:02:48.131 --> 01:02:49.820
that are currently
used in practice,

01:02:49.821 --> 01:02:52.795
they don't have bounds
on how much you lose,

01:02:52.796 --> 01:02:54.330
unless the loss-less ones,

01:02:54.331 --> 01:02:55.850
but they don't abstract enough.

01:02:55.851 --> 01:02:58.850
The remaining game is
still too big to solve.

01:02:59.100 --> 01:03:01.310
So, now, we have these lossy

01:03:01.311 --> 01:03:03.465
abstraction algorithms
with bounds.

01:03:03.466 --> 01:03:05.550
After we started
developing those,

01:03:05.551 --> 01:03:07.140
I realized that they're
not really about

01:03:07.141 --> 01:03:09.535
just abstraction for
computational speed.

01:03:09.536 --> 01:03:11.350
They're also the same theory

01:03:11.351 --> 01:03:13.585
applies to looking at modelling.

01:03:13.586 --> 01:03:15.910
So, all modelling is abstraction.

01:03:15.911 --> 01:03:17.640
Now this is talking about

01:03:17.641 --> 01:03:18.950
if we can measure the difference

01:03:18.951 --> 01:03:20.340
between reality or bound

01:03:20.341 --> 01:03:22.770
the difference between
reality and our model,

01:03:22.771 --> 01:03:24.210
then we can, for the first time,

01:03:24.211 --> 01:03:26.450
say that our game
theoretic strategies have

01:03:26.451 --> 01:03:29.145
some bound on how good they
are for the real world,

01:03:29.146 --> 01:03:30.730
which is kind of astonishing in

01:03:30.731 --> 01:03:34.990
that game theorists have been
doing game theory for many,

01:03:34.991 --> 01:03:38.455
many decades yet there
has been no discussion

01:03:38.456 --> 01:03:40.430
of how the answers
that come out or

01:03:40.431 --> 01:03:43.265
models actually how good
are they for reality.

01:03:43.266 --> 01:03:44.990
Now, to do still is,

01:03:44.991 --> 01:03:46.050
we need to unify

01:03:46.051 --> 01:03:47.740
our best practical abstraction

01:03:47.741 --> 01:03:49.475
algorithms with the theory.

01:03:49.476 --> 01:03:51.340
So, the theoretical
algorithms are not the

01:03:51.341 --> 01:03:54.180
same as the practically
best ones currently.

01:03:54.181 --> 01:03:57.245
Simultaneous abstraction
and equilibrium finding.

01:03:57.246 --> 01:03:59.660
So, I showed you
this waterflow of all,

01:03:59.661 --> 01:04:01.170
which is a traditional
way of doing it,

01:04:01.171 --> 01:04:03.140
abstract first then solve.

01:04:03.141 --> 01:04:05.605
Here, we are actually

01:04:05.606 --> 01:04:08.530
allowing the equilibrium
finder to tell

01:04:08.531 --> 01:04:11.370
what traits are similar
in their strategies and

01:04:11.371 --> 01:04:15.005
therefore maybe they
shouldn't be separated.

01:04:15.006 --> 01:04:17.260
Similarly, so
this an iteration between

01:04:17.261 --> 01:04:19.170
abstraction and
equilibrium finding,

01:04:19.171 --> 01:04:22.465
and the key then is how do
you actually warm start

01:04:22.466 --> 01:04:24.720
the equilibrium finder
once you've change

01:04:24.721 --> 01:04:27.015
your abstraction otherwise
it's way too slow.

01:04:27.016 --> 01:04:29.210
So, we first had

01:04:29.390 --> 01:04:32.260
warm starting algorithms
that relied on

01:04:32.261 --> 01:04:35.210
a particular kind of
equilibrium finding algorithm.

01:04:35.211 --> 01:04:39.065
Now, we can do general warm
starts for CFR algorithms.

01:04:39.066 --> 01:04:42.650
So, no matter where the
initial strategies came from,

01:04:42.651 --> 01:04:44.350
from a different
equilibrium finder,

01:04:44.351 --> 01:04:45.615
from human intuition,

01:04:45.616 --> 01:04:49.470
whatever, we can now
start CFR with those.

01:04:49.471 --> 01:04:51.340
So, you don't have to
start from scratch.

01:04:51.341 --> 01:04:54.800
CFR is a counterfactual
regret minimization.

01:04:55.580 --> 01:05:00.865
Then, new fastest algorithm
for the CFR family.

01:05:00.866 --> 01:05:03.940
So, turns out that
in the CFR family,

01:05:03.941 --> 01:05:07.435
CFR+ has for a few years now
been basically unbeatable,

01:05:07.436 --> 01:05:08.670
and a lot of people have

01:05:08.671 --> 01:05:10.240
tried to come up
with new variants of

01:05:10.241 --> 01:05:13.015
the CFR family and
they've all been

01:05:13.016 --> 01:05:16.570
either way slower than CFR+
or a little bit slower.

01:05:16.571 --> 01:05:18.820
But now, we have something as

01:05:18.821 --> 01:05:25.055
faster as we're planning
to submit within a month.

01:05:25.056 --> 01:05:27.490
Then, regret
decomposition framework.

01:05:27.491 --> 01:05:29.300
I mentioned that in CFR,

01:05:29.301 --> 01:05:33.505
you are actually
decomposing the regret,

01:05:33.506 --> 01:05:35.510
so that you can do
regret minimization

01:05:35.511 --> 01:05:37.710
separately into information sets.

01:05:37.711 --> 01:05:39.810
But turns out that
the way to do it

01:05:39.811 --> 01:05:41.685
in CFR is not the only way.

01:05:41.686 --> 01:05:44.260
There's actually
this infinite family

01:05:44.261 --> 01:05:46.675
of exact decompositions
you can do,

01:05:46.676 --> 01:05:49.020
and now you can go
beyond the CFR family.

01:05:49.021 --> 01:05:50.850
And again, there, you can get

01:05:50.851 --> 01:05:53.970
better algorithms than
in the CFR framework.

01:05:53.971 --> 01:05:56.560
Then, equilibrium-finding
algorithms by

01:05:56.561 --> 01:05:59.380
developing new theory of
gradient-based techniques.

01:05:59.381 --> 01:06:01.270
So, gradient-based
techniques have

01:06:01.271 --> 01:06:05.020
better worse case convergence
rates in CFR by a lot,

01:06:05.021 --> 01:06:08.225
but in practice, CFR
has been faster.

01:06:08.226 --> 01:06:11.215
It's kind of a mystery, and
it's still kind of true,

01:06:11.216 --> 01:06:14.100
but now we're within
a factor of four of

01:06:14.101 --> 01:06:18.515
CFR speed with these types
of algorithms as well.

01:06:18.516 --> 01:06:21.355
Algorithms for
equilibrium refinements.

01:06:21.356 --> 01:06:23.875
We talked about mostly
Nash equilibrium today.

01:06:23.876 --> 01:06:27.000
But Nash equilibrium
doesn't play optimally if

01:06:27.001 --> 01:06:28.585
the opponent or we

01:06:28.586 --> 01:06:31.160
have made a mistake
in the game so far.

01:06:31.161 --> 01:06:33.745
That's where equilibrium
refinements come in.

01:06:33.746 --> 01:06:36.605
They're basically
additional constraints

01:06:36.606 --> 01:06:39.430
on what counts as
a good solution.

01:06:39.431 --> 01:06:42.985
Just to pick one
here, this last one,

01:06:42.986 --> 01:06:46.110
trembling hand
refinements are maybe

01:06:46.111 --> 01:06:49.335
the most famous family of those,

01:06:49.336 --> 01:06:52.770
and they had been very
hard to compute even

01:06:52.771 --> 01:06:56.395
for tiny games and
numerically unstable.

01:06:56.396 --> 01:06:59.530
So, now, we have
algorithms that compute

01:06:59.531 --> 01:07:02.540
trembling hand refinements for

01:07:02.541 --> 01:07:05.985
games with about 10,000,
maybe 30,000 nodes.

01:07:05.986 --> 01:07:07.755
So, still way less scalable,

01:07:07.756 --> 01:07:09.250
but several orders of magnitude

01:07:09.251 --> 01:07:11.460
more scalable than before.

01:07:12.130 --> 01:07:15.490
Equilibrium-finding
algorithms for two agents,

01:07:15.491 --> 01:07:18.925
that's still way less scalable.

01:07:18.926 --> 01:07:23.400
We have the fastest one
currently for normal-form games.

01:07:23.401 --> 01:07:24.985
So, matrix form games,

01:07:24.986 --> 01:07:28.760
and how it works is that we're
doing a tree search not in

01:07:28.761 --> 01:07:30.490
the space of
the discrete supports of

01:07:30.491 --> 01:07:32.720
the actions as to what
actions are in play,

01:07:32.721 --> 01:07:35.625
like we and others
have tried before,

01:07:35.626 --> 01:07:38.625
but in the space of
the continuous probabilities.

01:07:38.626 --> 01:07:40.370
So, we basically split

01:07:40.371 --> 01:07:43.015
the probability space into
this hyper rectangles,

01:07:43.016 --> 01:07:45.530
and then we have an oracle
which checks whether

01:07:45.531 --> 01:07:47.230
hyper rectangle can potentially

01:07:47.231 --> 01:07:49.725
have an equilibrium in it,

01:07:49.726 --> 01:07:54.185
in this non-linear problem
of more than two agents.

01:07:54.186 --> 01:07:56.359
Then, for exploration,

01:07:56.360 --> 01:07:58.465
exploitation versus
exploitability.

01:07:58.466 --> 01:08:00.180
I've had this on the slide for-

01:08:00.181 --> 01:08:02.020
in my thoughts for
a long time now,

01:08:02.021 --> 01:08:03.550
and we still haven't
really cracked it,

01:08:03.551 --> 01:08:06.065
but I can say a few
interesting things about it.

01:08:06.066 --> 01:08:09.590
So, in reinforcement learning,

01:08:09.591 --> 01:08:10.260
there's this trade-off

01:08:10.261 --> 01:08:12.295
between exploration
and exploitation.

01:08:12.296 --> 01:08:13.660
In games, there's also

01:08:13.661 --> 01:08:16.215
this third dimension
of exploitability.

01:08:16.216 --> 01:08:17.960
As I start to exploit,

01:08:17.961 --> 01:08:21.795
how much do I open myself
up to exploitation?

01:08:21.796 --> 01:08:25.500
The folk wisdom
had been that if I

01:08:25.501 --> 01:08:29.275
exploit more than any game
theoretic strategy would,

01:08:29.276 --> 01:08:31.125
any Nash equilibrium
strategy would,

01:08:31.126 --> 01:08:33.850
then I'm opening myself
to exploitation.

01:08:33.851 --> 01:08:38.220
Turns out not true. You can
actually completely safely

01:08:38.221 --> 01:08:39.750
exploit your opponent
by more than

01:08:39.751 --> 01:08:42.760
that up to a bound
which we characterize.

01:08:42.761 --> 01:08:46.045
It's actually has
a nice intuition in that,

01:08:46.046 --> 01:08:47.860
I can't really- well,

01:08:47.861 --> 01:08:49.505
let me say what
doesn't work first.

01:08:49.506 --> 01:08:51.770
So, you might think that I
can take the winnings that I

01:08:51.771 --> 01:08:53.935
have from Eric so far,

01:08:53.936 --> 01:08:56.095
and I can bankroll
my exploitation.

01:08:56.096 --> 01:08:57.895
I can exploit him so much

01:08:57.896 --> 01:09:02.050
that I might lose up to
what I've already won.

01:09:02.051 --> 01:09:04.310
Seems like that might be safe,

01:09:04.311 --> 01:09:06.930
but it's not safe
because I'm walking with

01:09:06.931 --> 01:09:09.530
my upside and my downside

01:09:09.531 --> 01:09:12.165
from an ex-ante perspective
is still all there.

01:09:12.166 --> 01:09:16.080
So, I need to be able to
separate out what I've

01:09:16.081 --> 01:09:19.700
won based on luck
versus Eric's mistakes.

01:09:19.701 --> 01:09:21.450
If I can lower bound how much

01:09:21.451 --> 01:09:23.425
I've won based on
Eric's mistakes,

01:09:23.426 --> 01:09:25.520
that is the amount that I can

01:09:25.521 --> 01:09:29.380
use to bankroll
my exploitation in the future.

01:09:29.381 --> 01:09:32.290
Then, a final thing on
this slide is depth

01:09:32.291 --> 01:09:35.035
limited solving in
imperfect information games.

01:09:35.036 --> 01:09:37.070
So, we talked about
sub-game solving,

01:09:37.071 --> 01:09:39.040
but that was solving
the end of the game.

01:09:39.041 --> 01:09:40.810
Now, we can actually
solve the beginning

01:09:40.811 --> 01:09:43.030
and the middle as
well on the fly.

01:09:43.031 --> 01:09:46.660
The idea that doesn't
work here is to say,

01:09:46.661 --> 01:09:49.350
that I'm going to fix
the opponent strategy at

01:09:49.351 --> 01:09:51.050
the leaf to what
they would play in

01:09:51.051 --> 01:09:54.430
the blueprint because that
gives me too much power.

01:09:54.431 --> 01:09:56.800
Instead, I'm going
to give the opponent

01:09:56.801 --> 01:10:00.030
one final action at each leaf.

01:10:00.031 --> 01:10:02.400
It's each leaf information set

01:10:02.401 --> 01:10:07.130
and there they can choose
which strategy to play.

01:10:07.131 --> 01:10:11.205
That takes away that extra power
and that is the idea

01:10:11.206 --> 01:10:12.770
based on which we
can do depth limited

01:10:12.771 --> 01:10:15.385
solving in imperfect
information games.

01:10:15.386 --> 01:10:18.930
And these are still
theoretical guarantees and this

01:10:18.931 --> 01:10:23.550
created the bot
moricom in my lab,

01:10:23.551 --> 01:10:26.035
which was created on one server,

01:10:26.036 --> 01:10:26.840
and to our knowledge,

01:10:26.841 --> 01:10:29.240
it's the second best AI
for this problem.

01:10:29.241 --> 01:10:31.415
All right. So,
commercial applications.

01:10:31.416 --> 01:10:33.020
Well, I think that
most real-world settings

01:10:33.021 --> 01:10:34.910
are imperfect information games,

01:10:34.911 --> 01:10:36.905
although they're not
treated as such.

01:10:36.906 --> 01:10:39.330
Now, that we have
this type of technology,

01:10:39.331 --> 01:10:41.950
I think we're going to start
to see applications where

01:10:41.951 --> 01:10:45.300
things are really treated
as games for what they are.

01:10:45.301 --> 01:10:47.945
So, one example is
strategic pricing.

01:10:47.946 --> 01:10:49.960
Typically, when
companies do pricing,

01:10:49.961 --> 01:10:51.440
they optimize their prices

01:10:51.441 --> 01:10:53.890
given their competitors
price is fixed.

01:10:53.891 --> 01:10:56.700
Single-agent problem, but
in reality, of course,

01:10:56.701 --> 01:10:58.395
the competitors change the prices

01:10:58.396 --> 01:10:59.120
and you don't want to be

01:10:59.121 --> 01:11:02.175
just pre-optimizing to
that and being reactive,

01:11:02.176 --> 01:11:04.170
you want to think about
these things ahead.

01:11:04.171 --> 01:11:07.600
Same in strategic product
portfolio optimization.

01:11:07.601 --> 01:11:10.710
Gaming, video games,
training, streams,

01:11:10.711 --> 01:11:12.640
you can make really
exciting streams by having

01:11:12.641 --> 01:11:14.110
these superhuman AIs play against

01:11:14.111 --> 01:11:15.840
each other. Bot detection.

01:11:15.841 --> 01:11:18.950
Turns out that they have moves
that you must never play,

01:11:18.951 --> 01:11:20.845
and there are millions of them,

01:11:20.846 --> 01:11:23.590
and you can detect bots not

01:11:23.591 --> 01:11:26.080
just by looking at
the operating system level,

01:11:26.081 --> 01:11:28.510
but really from
the strategies themselves.

01:11:28.511 --> 01:11:32.570
Automated negotiation,
electricity markets,

01:11:32.571 --> 01:11:35.790
various aspects of finance,
investment banking,

01:11:35.791 --> 01:11:37.630
political campaigns,
how do you spend media

01:11:37.631 --> 01:11:39.980
against an adversary,
business strategy.

01:11:39.981 --> 01:11:41.265
Hey, wouldn't it be
nice if there were

01:11:41.266 --> 01:11:43.870
actually some strategy
in business strategy?

01:11:43.871 --> 01:11:46.170
Bidding. How do you bid?

01:11:46.171 --> 01:11:47.935
For example for oil tracts,

01:11:47.936 --> 01:11:50.240
and then, once you
know how to bid,

01:11:50.241 --> 01:11:51.270
you can actually evaluate

01:11:51.271 --> 01:11:52.550
auction designs that you couldn't

01:11:52.551 --> 01:11:55.700
normally evaluate because
you cancel for equilibrium.

01:11:55.701 --> 01:11:57.840
Strategic market segmentation.

01:11:57.841 --> 01:11:59.335
Same thing as with pricing.

01:11:59.336 --> 01:12:01.030
Typically, market
segmentation has

01:12:01.031 --> 01:12:02.675
been created as a static problem.

01:12:02.676 --> 01:12:03.760
Of course, you have to think

01:12:03.761 --> 01:12:04.960
about strategically how would

01:12:04.961 --> 01:12:08.455
the competitors change
their strategies.

01:12:08.456 --> 01:12:12.540
Change their segmentation
in the future.

01:12:12.541 --> 01:12:15.640
Interaction between vehicles
and autonomous fleets.

01:12:15.641 --> 01:12:18.100
So, let's say that you have

01:12:18.101 --> 01:12:21.515
two operators of
autonomous fleets in the future.

01:12:21.516 --> 01:12:25.520
The rules of the road put
limits on what they can do,

01:12:25.521 --> 01:12:27.240
but there are a lot of
unspecified things,

01:12:27.241 --> 01:12:29.345
like how do we
merge, for example.

01:12:29.346 --> 01:12:31.620
Wouldn't it be nice if we
wouldn't have to negotiate

01:12:31.621 --> 01:12:34.480
those in some ad
hoc way on the fly,

01:12:34.481 --> 01:12:36.770
but there are too many
negotiation situations

01:12:36.771 --> 01:12:38.590
to manually negotiate in advance.

01:12:38.591 --> 01:12:40.885
So, you would want to
somehow automate that.

01:12:40.886 --> 01:12:43.445
Acquisition strategy
like movie rights.

01:12:43.446 --> 01:12:45.940
How should streaming
company buy movies,

01:12:45.941 --> 01:12:47.500
which movies should they bid for,

01:12:47.501 --> 01:12:48.580
in what order should they

01:12:48.581 --> 01:12:49.870
buy them from the movie studios,

01:12:49.871 --> 01:12:53.080
what should they offer, and
so on. Sports of course.

01:12:53.081 --> 01:12:58.050
Lots of sports applications
and maybe the most radical,

01:12:58.051 --> 01:13:00.690
it's got a long story,
but steering evolution

01:13:00.691 --> 01:13:02.670
and biological adaptation
strategically.

01:13:02.671 --> 01:13:03.760
For example, for medical

01:13:03.761 --> 01:13:05.850
treatment planning or
synthetic biology.

01:13:05.851 --> 01:13:08.835
Then there's a bunch of
government applications.

01:13:08.836 --> 01:13:10.515
So, if you think about it,

01:13:10.516 --> 01:13:15.705
traditionally in
military planning,

01:13:15.706 --> 01:13:17.160
whether it's cybersecurity or

01:13:17.161 --> 01:13:19.175
actual physical
military or whatever,

01:13:19.176 --> 01:13:21.615
you assume a naive opponent,

01:13:21.616 --> 01:13:23.640
a naive red, a fixed strategy.

01:13:23.641 --> 01:13:25.790
You assign a strategy
and then you simulate

01:13:25.791 --> 01:13:27.985
against that or do
war gaming against that.

01:13:27.986 --> 01:13:30.225
Here, unlike simulation,

01:13:30.226 --> 01:13:33.360
red strategy is not input
but is rather output.

01:13:33.361 --> 01:13:34.835
So, you're actually looking at

01:13:34.836 --> 01:13:36.290
all of the possible opponents

01:13:36.291 --> 01:13:39.155
strategies and so you're robust,

01:13:39.156 --> 01:13:42.095
and that is a bunch of
applications. Thank you very much.

01:13:42.096 --> 01:13:52.090
&gt;&gt; In curiosity, you would

01:13:52.091 --> 01:13:55.560
need your subgame innovations to

01:13:55.561 --> 01:14:03.160
approximate good jobs in
those open world, messy spaces.

01:14:03.161 --> 01:14:05.270
It seems more of it's
in generically looking

01:14:05.271 --> 01:14:07.400
at game theoretic
approaches in these spaces,

01:14:07.401 --> 01:14:11.050
or have you seen
the actual application of

01:14:11.051 --> 01:14:13.270
the nuances of the way you solve

01:14:13.271 --> 01:14:15.940
the two player poker game?

01:14:15.941 --> 01:14:16.795
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:14:16.796 --> 01:14:18.270
&gt;&gt; That's used for an open world.

01:14:18.271 --> 01:14:22.565
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, there's always
a question of modeling,

01:14:22.566 --> 01:14:23.690
and when we think about

01:14:23.691 --> 01:14:25.420
business strategy for
these two startups,

01:14:25.421 --> 01:14:27.800
we're looking at what
are the closest,

01:14:27.801 --> 01:14:29.065
where we can say, "Okay,

01:14:29.066 --> 01:14:30.465
well, we have pretty
good models."

01:14:30.466 --> 01:14:32.450
You start from there.
Good models and

01:14:32.451 --> 01:14:34.460
close to zero sum or
exactly zero sum.

01:14:34.461 --> 01:14:36.060
So, you're really looking at

01:14:36.061 --> 01:14:40.930
very adversarial situations,
high-stakes, of course,

01:14:40.931 --> 01:14:46.355
and once where the
Rumsfeld unknown unknowns

01:14:46.356 --> 01:14:49.810
are not that big a deal.

01:14:49.811 --> 01:14:50.920
But now, modeling is really,

01:14:50.921 --> 01:14:52.740
I would say the number
one bottleneck here,

01:14:52.741 --> 01:14:54.720
is that if the real world is

01:14:54.721 --> 01:14:56.975
such that you can't really
model it accurately,

01:14:56.976 --> 01:14:59.115
that is the number
one show stopper.

01:14:59.116 --> 01:15:01.630
&gt;&gt; It's like a pretty big show
stopping for what?

01:15:01.631 --> 01:15:02.500
&gt;&gt; Real world application?

01:15:02.501 --> 01:15:04.550
&gt;&gt; Well, I'm not sure.

01:15:04.551 --> 01:15:06.920
There's infinitely
many real world applications

01:15:06.921 --> 01:15:08.760
and probably infinitely
many that you can model.

01:15:08.761 --> 01:15:10.280
So, while it's a worry,

01:15:10.281 --> 01:15:11.585
I'm not really worried.

01:15:11.586 --> 01:15:13.715
The other is the
abstraction techniques.

01:15:13.716 --> 01:15:15.725
Now, if you can bound

01:15:15.726 --> 01:15:17.950
the difference between
the real world and the model,

01:15:17.951 --> 01:15:19.460
you can say that, "Hey,

01:15:19.461 --> 01:15:21.030
even if you solve just the model,

01:15:21.031 --> 01:15:24.200
you're close optimal
for the real world."

01:15:24.201 --> 01:15:26.225
&gt;&gt; Okay. Open
questions. Yeah, Paul.

01:15:26.226 --> 01:15:28.070
&gt;&gt; So, part of

01:15:28.071 --> 01:15:30.005
the game theory approaches
the idea that even if

01:15:30.006 --> 01:15:31.790
your opponent knows your policy

01:15:31.791 --> 01:15:35.225
right, they wouldn't
change anything.

01:15:35.226 --> 01:15:37.770
Have you tried this with the
humans in the sense that,

01:15:37.771 --> 01:15:40.215
if you have a probability
of a range of hands,

01:15:40.216 --> 01:15:42.070
then you can actually
expose that to

01:15:42.071 --> 01:15:44.370
the humans because they still
know what action you took,

01:15:44.371 --> 01:15:45.530
and actually start reflecting

01:15:45.531 --> 01:15:47.620
the internal state of
the bot to them, right?

01:15:47.621 --> 01:15:48.590
So, the bot would actually say,

01:15:48.591 --> 01:15:49.720
"Here's the hands I puts you on."

01:15:49.721 --> 01:15:51.090
That's effectively a summary of

01:15:51.091 --> 01:15:52.720
the policy from the point
of view of them.

01:15:52.721 --> 01:15:54.790
They don't know what
actually took after that.

01:15:54.791 --> 01:15:56.120
Have you tried to
seeing that and see how

01:15:56.121 --> 01:15:57.630
they adapt or learn
based on that?

01:15:57.631 --> 01:16:02.655
&gt;&gt; No, but we've done
something similar.

01:16:02.656 --> 01:16:04.740
Eric and I were chatting a little

01:16:04.741 --> 01:16:06.410
bit about this on
the fireside chat.

01:16:06.411 --> 01:16:11.240
So, again, this comes to
people believing in people.

01:16:11.241 --> 01:16:13.600
So, we got in the poker already

01:16:13.601 --> 01:16:16.310
before this event for
the last few years before that,

01:16:16.311 --> 01:16:17.495
the last couple at least.

01:16:17.496 --> 01:16:19.500
It was a situation where when

01:16:19.501 --> 01:16:21.450
human expert poker players
would tell us,

01:16:21.451 --> 01:16:23.840
"Okay, they're bot
both made a mistake."

01:16:23.841 --> 01:16:26.090
Chances were much
higher than they were

01:16:26.091 --> 01:16:29.005
wrong than that the bot
had made a mistake.

01:16:29.006 --> 01:16:32.800
What we did, I had
Chris Ferguson,

01:16:32.801 --> 01:16:36.420
who's a really kind of
a mathematical PhD level guy,

01:16:36.421 --> 01:16:38.250
who's a top poker player,

01:16:38.251 --> 01:16:39.570
look at these situations where

01:16:39.571 --> 01:16:41.565
somebody told us that
"Hey, that was a mistake."

01:16:41.566 --> 01:16:44.170
Then we would give him
the belief distributions,

01:16:44.171 --> 01:16:46.750
and the path, and everything.

01:16:46.751 --> 01:16:48.740
He would actually write
these little essays

01:16:48.741 --> 01:16:51.515
that tell what happen
in every time.

01:16:51.516 --> 01:16:54.550
It's just like
super human reasoning.

01:16:54.551 --> 01:16:55.850
In his words, he was thinking

01:16:55.851 --> 01:16:57.590
like two levels deeper
than any human,

01:16:57.591 --> 01:16:59.745
and at once he had three days
to think about it.

01:16:59.746 --> 01:17:02.350
Actually, that was
a brilliant move, not a mistake.

01:17:02.351 --> 01:17:04.325
But sometimes, they look weird,

01:17:04.326 --> 01:17:06.825
like one happened
during the play.

01:17:06.826 --> 01:17:11.040
We had the AI called,

01:17:11.041 --> 01:17:14.750
I think maybe it was all
in or some other huge,

01:17:14.751 --> 01:17:18.960
huge pot called, a huge
bet with Jack High.

01:17:18.961 --> 01:17:21.550
Most people who play
poker would say,

01:17:21.551 --> 01:17:23.455
"I thought they, well,
that was a mistake."

01:17:23.456 --> 01:17:25.360
But turns out that exactly with

01:17:25.361 --> 01:17:26.820
that Jack and that other card,

01:17:26.821 --> 01:17:28.600
maybe it was a three or whatever.

01:17:28.601 --> 01:17:31.185
That is exactly with what you do,

01:17:31.186 --> 01:17:33.385
you play the light call
just with that,

01:17:33.386 --> 01:17:34.780
because you have
to light call with

01:17:34.781 --> 01:17:36.440
something and with the blockers,

01:17:36.441 --> 01:17:38.605
that was exactly the antidote
to light call with.

01:17:38.606 --> 01:17:39.880
You don't do it with 100 percent,

01:17:39.881 --> 01:17:42.820
you do it for some percent,
which was not near zero,

01:17:42.821 --> 01:17:45.460
but once he really
thought that through,

01:17:45.461 --> 01:17:49.110
he actually saw
the merit in that.

01:17:51.320 --> 01:17:53.665
&gt;&gt; Is there a rule of machine

01:17:53.666 --> 01:17:54.925
learning like you're listening,

01:17:54.926 --> 01:18:01.940
like your doctor or
[inaudible] right?

01:18:01.941 --> 01:18:06.530
Now, you have this
really good poker players.

01:18:06.690 --> 01:18:10.585
Does it help you, for
example, in addition to that,

01:18:10.586 --> 01:18:12.795
like you're solving it
in a very strong manner,

01:18:12.796 --> 01:18:14.880
but does it help in compute,

01:18:14.881 --> 01:18:17.060
maybe like the first part

01:18:17.061 --> 01:18:18.090
when you're doing
the abstraction,

01:18:18.091 --> 01:18:20.610
and then there's
many months or when you use

01:18:20.611 --> 01:18:23.610
thousand CPU cycles to drive

01:18:23.611 --> 01:18:27.070
like learn from expert
for demonstrations,

01:18:27.071 --> 01:18:32.730
and then do the compute upwards
such seated right back,

01:18:32.731 --> 01:18:36.030
or is that not still
a viable strategy?

01:18:36.031 --> 01:18:39.125
&gt;&gt; I see at least three roles.

01:18:39.126 --> 01:18:44.050
One is that, we now have
the reef warm starts techniques.

01:18:44.051 --> 01:18:46.480
So, you can actually warm
start from any strategy,

01:18:46.481 --> 01:18:47.590
no matter how it's computed.

01:18:47.591 --> 01:18:49.250
Whether it's computed
with machine learning

01:18:49.251 --> 01:18:51.290
or some guy just thought
that that was a good idea.

01:18:51.291 --> 01:18:53.720
It doesn't matter. We can
warm start from that.

01:18:53.721 --> 01:18:56.390
The second is that,

01:18:56.391 --> 01:18:58.370
when we do opponent exploitation.

01:18:58.371 --> 01:19:01.180
Those techniques, which
we didn't use here,

01:19:01.181 --> 01:19:03.640
but those techniques you
could think of those as

01:19:03.641 --> 01:19:05.460
machine learning
techniques which are

01:19:05.461 --> 01:19:07.740
kind of fortified by game
theoretic reasoning,

01:19:07.741 --> 01:19:09.600
and there's definitely a role

01:19:09.601 --> 01:19:11.960
for machine learning and data.

01:19:12.560 --> 01:19:15.640
Then the third is the modelling.

01:19:15.641 --> 01:19:17.635
So, I have to believe

01:19:17.636 --> 01:19:20.735
that machine learning which
is all backward-looking,

01:19:20.736 --> 01:19:23.840
and strategic reasoning which
is all forward-looking.

01:19:23.841 --> 01:19:26.095
They have to be
somehow synergistic.

01:19:26.096 --> 01:19:29.420
One of the ways that those
are synergistic, I think,

01:19:29.421 --> 01:19:34.055
is that machine learning can
be used to learn the model,

01:19:34.056 --> 01:19:35.450
or aspects of the model.

01:19:35.451 --> 01:19:37.390
Like for example,
chance probabilities

01:19:37.391 --> 01:19:38.715
which are not strategic

01:19:38.716 --> 01:19:43.250
or what moves are plausible,
or things like that.

01:19:43.500 --> 01:19:46.050
&gt;&gt; Have you tried

01:19:46.051 --> 01:19:47.590
to bring this system
yet in the tournament

01:19:47.591 --> 01:19:49.160
where your early rounds
at tournaments

01:19:49.161 --> 01:19:50.760
are all about exploitation,

01:19:50.761 --> 01:19:53.475
so that you can chip up and
then have the chip advantage.

01:19:53.476 --> 01:19:55.030
How does it actually performs

01:19:55.031 --> 01:19:56.770
in a multiplayer situations?

01:19:56.771 --> 01:19:58.640
&gt;&gt; Yeah. We we
haven't really played

01:19:58.641 --> 01:20:00.410
tournaments and we haven't
played multiplayer.

01:20:00.411 --> 01:20:01.900
We have a couple of players,

01:20:01.901 --> 01:20:03.845
early papers a long time ago

01:20:03.846 --> 01:20:07.670
on three-player jump
fall tournaments,

01:20:07.671 --> 01:20:09.820
like three player in
games of tournaments.

01:20:09.821 --> 01:20:11.340
But we haven't really looked at

01:20:11.341 --> 01:20:13.700
that tournament play per say.

01:20:13.701 --> 01:20:15.000
One thing I can say is that,

01:20:15.001 --> 01:20:18.160
when it comes to not tournaments
but multiplayer,

01:20:18.161 --> 01:20:20.855
I hear from a lot of botters.

01:20:20.856 --> 01:20:22.580
Not academics but botters.

01:20:22.581 --> 01:20:26.440
That the way they generate
the top ports today,

01:20:26.441 --> 01:20:27.820
for let's say six max.

01:20:27.821 --> 01:20:29.475
They're very much
the same techniques.

01:20:29.476 --> 01:20:31.000
So, while in theory,

01:20:31.001 --> 01:20:32.570
there's this big complexity gap

01:20:32.571 --> 01:20:34.205
between two players
zero sum games,

01:20:34.206 --> 01:20:37.050
and more general games,
in practice,

01:20:37.051 --> 01:20:39.610
at least for poker, it seems
that the same techniques do

01:20:39.611 --> 01:20:42.720
well even in those
more general settings.

01:20:42.721 --> 01:20:46.090
Yeah.

01:20:46.091 --> 01:20:48.795
&gt;&gt; So, your original blueprints

01:20:48.796 --> 01:20:53.175
is over some abstraction
but it's Nash, right?

01:20:53.176 --> 01:20:57.395
&gt;&gt; It's an approximate Nash
for some abstraction, yes.

01:20:57.396 --> 01:21:00.310
&gt;&gt; So, even in that,

01:21:00.311 --> 01:21:04.045
I guess for any coarse
or fine abstraction,

01:21:04.046 --> 01:21:05.220
at least in poker,

01:21:05.221 --> 01:21:08.925
it seems once you
have a good model,

01:21:08.926 --> 01:21:11.150
the right move is,

01:21:11.151 --> 01:21:13.120
I wouldn't say immediate but at

01:21:13.121 --> 01:21:14.630
least computable
or you could say,

01:21:14.631 --> 01:21:16.350
"This is the right move
distribution."

01:21:16.351 --> 01:21:17.860
But I guess in real life,

01:21:17.861 --> 01:21:20.930
even if I know the right move
is to exit the room,

01:21:20.931 --> 01:21:22.450
it takes a lot of actuator to

01:21:22.451 --> 01:21:25.300
actually affect that goal, right?

01:21:25.301 --> 01:21:29.870
So, like I guess there are
settings where you have to

01:21:29.871 --> 01:21:30.930
go but it takes

01:21:30.931 --> 01:21:34.540
some extra mileage to actually
reach that goal, right?

01:21:34.541 --> 01:21:35.740
I think in poker, it's more

01:21:35.741 --> 01:21:36.950
immediate where I need to make

01:21:36.951 --> 01:21:39.950
this move and you could directly
affect that move for it.

01:21:39.951 --> 01:21:42.345
But I think, how do
you reason about that,

01:21:42.346 --> 01:21:43.820
where even if the model of

01:21:43.821 --> 01:21:45.550
the world is taken
to reach some goal,

01:21:45.551 --> 01:21:47.280
there needs to be
some kind of planning or

01:21:47.281 --> 01:21:48.560
learning how to actually perform

01:21:48.561 --> 01:21:49.630
the action that do it, right?

01:21:49.631 --> 01:21:51.790
It's kind of like, "I
know the best thing to do

01:21:51.791 --> 01:21:54.520
is if I take my three point
shots here but I can't do it,

01:21:54.521 --> 01:21:56.960
because I cannot make
the shot very right."

01:21:56.961 --> 01:21:58.330
How do you reason about that?

01:21:58.331 --> 01:22:00.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Well, we don't.

01:22:00.171 --> 01:22:03.160
You're right, we don't.
When we take an action,

01:22:03.161 --> 01:22:04.260
it's not like robotics where

01:22:04.261 --> 01:22:05.400
there's slippage and whatever.

01:22:05.401 --> 01:22:05.940
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:22:05.941 --> 01:22:08.015
&gt;&gt; It's like when we take
an action, that action happens.

01:22:08.016 --> 01:22:08.560
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:22:08.561 --> 01:22:10.750
&gt;&gt; But I'll take issue
with the first part

01:22:10.751 --> 01:22:12.710
which is at somehow when
you get the abstraction,

01:22:12.711 --> 01:22:15.370
it somehow immediate with
the probability should be.

01:22:15.371 --> 01:22:17.890
That's very hard to compute

01:22:17.891 --> 01:22:20.830
with algorithms that had
been developed over 14,

01:22:20.831 --> 01:22:22.990
15 years on a
supercomputer for months.

01:22:22.991 --> 01:22:24.105
So, it's not immediate.

01:22:24.106 --> 01:22:26.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but it's
computable, right?

01:22:26.111 --> 01:22:29.480
So, I mean there will be
cases for this system,

01:22:29.481 --> 01:22:30.810
no matter how much you compute

01:22:30.811 --> 01:22:32.015
this, it's non-computable, right?

01:22:32.016 --> 01:22:34.610
How do you reason about them?

01:22:34.611 --> 01:22:37.490
&gt;&gt; Yes. I'm personally

01:22:37.491 --> 01:22:40.070
not that interested
in that question.

01:22:40.071 --> 01:22:40.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I asked.

01:22:40.601 --> 01:22:41.395
&gt;&gt; But I can tell you

01:22:41.396 --> 01:22:43.600
some things that people
have done for this,

01:22:43.601 --> 01:22:45.570
for example, game

01:22:45.571 --> 01:22:48.180
theoretic strategies under
execution uncertainty.

01:22:48.181 --> 01:22:49.555
There are a few papers on that,

01:22:49.556 --> 01:22:51.455
for example, for Bill Gates.

01:22:51.456 --> 01:22:52.435
&gt;&gt; I see.

01:22:52.436 --> 01:22:53.845
&gt;&gt; But not from my group.

01:22:53.846 --> 01:22:54.670
&gt;&gt; Okay.

01:22:54.671 --> 01:22:55.435
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

01:22:55.436 --> 01:22:57.160
&gt;&gt; Let's stop there,
and thank you.

01:22:57.161 --> 01:22:58.670
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

